WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.960 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:01.960 --> 00:00:03.430 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:03.430 --> 00:00:06.370 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:06.370 --> 00:00:11.760 align:middle line:90%


00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:12.760 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: OK.

00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:17.920 align:middle line:84%
So last time, we ended on
one of these cliffhangers.

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:21.520 align:middle line:84%
And so I rushed you through
a bunch of Japanese data.

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:26.185 align:middle line:84%
And then we got to this new
bit of linguistic technology--

00:00:26.185 --> 00:00:28.720 align:middle line:84%
[BUZZING] that's
going to be annoying--

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:30.400 align:middle line:84%
this new bit of
linguistic technology

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:33.310 align:middle line:90%
and went through it pretty fast.

00:00:33.310 --> 00:00:37.510 align:middle line:84%
So what I want to do is do the
run up to that again a little

00:00:37.510 --> 00:00:40.888 align:middle line:84%
more carefully and show you what
it is that we're talking about.

00:00:40.888 --> 00:00:42.430 align:middle line:84%
What we were talking
about at the end

00:00:42.430 --> 00:00:46.430 align:middle line:84%
was the position of accent
in Japanese compounds.

00:00:46.430 --> 00:00:50.690 align:middle line:84%
So here are a bunch of
compound nouns in Japanese.

00:00:50.690 --> 00:00:52.930 align:middle line:84%
And the observation
was that when

00:00:52.930 --> 00:00:58.780 align:middle line:84%
you have a compound
consisting of two parts,

00:00:58.780 --> 00:01:00.700 align:middle line:84%
the result always
has one accent.

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:03.790 align:middle line:84%
It has accent on one
particular syllable.

00:01:03.790 --> 00:01:06.040 align:middle line:84%
The accented
syllable doesn't have

00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:10.480 align:middle line:84%
to be the accented syllable in
either of the original parts

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:11.360 align:middle line:90%
of the compound.

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:15.130 align:middle line:84%
So "raw egg," which is the
first example in this slide--

00:01:15.130 --> 00:01:19.020 align:middle line:84%
"raw" and "egg"--
they have accents

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:20.020 align:middle line:90%
where they have accents.

00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:22.180 align:middle line:84%
"Raw" has an accent
on the first syllable.

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:24.100 align:middle line:84%
"Egg" has an accent on
the second syllable.

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:27.100 align:middle line:84%
But "raw egg" has an accent
on the syllable which is not

00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:29.830 align:middle line:90%
normally accented in "egg."

00:01:29.830 --> 00:01:33.940 align:middle line:84%
What we said was there is
a set of rules for where

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:36.430 align:middle line:90%
accent goes in these compounds.

00:01:36.430 --> 00:01:39.310 align:middle line:84%
And the rules go
something like this.

00:01:39.310 --> 00:01:41.380 align:middle line:84%
There's only going
to be one accent.

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:45.370 align:middle line:84%
It needs to be next to the
boundary between the words.

00:01:45.370 --> 00:01:47.860 align:middle line:84%
And if possible, it should
be on the second word.

00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:51.040 align:middle line:84%
That is, it should be after
the boundary between the words.

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:54.100 align:middle line:84%
But you don't do that if that
would result in the accent

00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:55.450 align:middle line:90%
being on the last syllable.

00:01:55.450 --> 00:01:59.110 align:middle line:84%
So the general pattern is the
one you're getting in "French

00:01:59.110 --> 00:02:01.540 align:middle line:84%
fry" up there--
"furaido POteto"--

00:02:01.540 --> 00:02:04.960 align:middle line:84%
where you get accent at the
beginning of the second half

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:06.310 align:middle line:90%
of the compound, "potato."

00:02:06.310 --> 00:02:07.930 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:02:07.930 --> 00:02:10.539 align:middle line:84%
That's what you get in most of
the examples on the preceding

00:02:10.539 --> 00:02:11.039 align:middle line:90%
slide.

00:02:11.039 --> 00:02:12.890 align:middle line:84%
If we go back to
the preceding slide,

00:02:12.890 --> 00:02:14.498 align:middle line:84%
you can see that in
"raw egg," right?

00:02:14.498 --> 00:02:15.790 align:middle line:90%
It's at the beginning of "egg."

00:02:15.790 --> 00:02:17.020 align:middle line:84%
And you can see it
in "field mouse."

00:02:17.020 --> 00:02:18.395 align:middle line:84%
It's at the
beginning of "mouse."

00:02:18.395 --> 00:02:21.340 align:middle line:84%
And you're also getting
it in "French fry."

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:25.180 align:middle line:84%
But there's this coherent
class of exceptions.

00:02:25.180 --> 00:02:27.610 align:middle line:84%
If the second half
of the compound

00:02:27.610 --> 00:02:29.830 align:middle line:84%
is only one syllable
long, then you

00:02:29.830 --> 00:02:31.390 align:middle line:84%
don't put accent
on the second half.

00:02:31.390 --> 00:02:33.920 align:middle line:84%
You put it at the end
of the first half.

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:38.710 align:middle line:84%
So Kagawa prefecture-- "kagawa"
is the name of a place.

00:02:38.710 --> 00:02:41.410 align:middle line:84%
And it's got accent
on its first syllable.

00:02:41.410 --> 00:02:44.560 align:middle line:84%
But the expression for
"Kagawa prefecture"

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:47.467 align:middle line:84%
has accent at almost
at the boundary.

00:02:47.467 --> 00:02:49.300 align:middle line:84%
It's right before the
boundary between them,

00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:52.025 align:middle line:84%
not on the second half of
the compound, unusually,

00:02:52.025 --> 00:02:53.650 align:middle line:84%
because the second
half of the compound

00:02:53.650 --> 00:02:54.760 align:middle line:90%
is only one syllable long.

00:02:54.760 --> 00:02:56.853 align:middle line:90%
So we get "kagaWAken."

00:02:56.853 --> 00:02:59.220 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:02.010 align:middle line:84%
I'm telling you all this
not just to educate you

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:05.430 align:middle line:84%
in what a cool and complicated
language Japanese is,

00:03:05.430 --> 00:03:07.560 align:middle line:84%
but to show you
that there are cases

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:09.930 align:middle line:84%
of cool and
complicated phenomena

00:03:09.930 --> 00:03:13.440 align:middle line:84%
that can be fruitfully
thought of as a series

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:16.380 align:middle line:84%
of things that you're trying
to do which are in conflict

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:17.880 align:middle line:90%
with each other sometimes.

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:22.740 align:middle line:84%
So Japanese wants the accent
to be on the second word

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:24.810 align:middle line:84%
and next to the boundary
between the words.

00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:27.690 align:middle line:84%
But it also wants
it not to be final.

00:03:27.690 --> 00:03:31.650 align:middle line:84%
And if it can't have all
of those things at once,

00:03:31.650 --> 00:03:33.810 align:middle line:84%
like in "Kagawa
prefecture," it has

00:03:33.810 --> 00:03:36.780 align:middle line:84%
to decide, do I care more
about it not being final,

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:38.970 align:middle line:84%
or do I care more about it
being on the second word?

00:03:38.970 --> 00:03:42.930 align:middle line:84%
And Japanese cares more about
it not being final, right?

00:03:42.930 --> 00:03:44.640 align:middle line:84%
So it retracts it
from the second word

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:47.468 align:middle line:90%
to the end of the first word.

00:03:47.468 --> 00:03:48.510 align:middle line:90%
Does anybody still read--

00:03:48.510 --> 00:03:51.530 align:middle line:84%
Isaac Asimov had these
stories, the robot stories.

00:03:51.530 --> 00:03:53.280 align:middle line:90%
He had a book, I, Robot.

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:54.722 align:middle line:90%
It was a series of stories.

00:03:54.722 --> 00:03:56.430 align:middle line:84%
Some of you have heard
of them, at least?

00:03:56.430 --> 00:03:56.930 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:03:56.930 --> 00:04:01.830 align:middle line:84%
So they feature robots that
have these laws governing

00:04:01.830 --> 00:04:02.760 align:middle line:90%
how they work.

00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:04.980 align:middle line:84%
And the laws act like
these kinds of laws.

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:06.870 align:middle line:90%
So there's the first law.

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:08.340 align:middle line:90%
And then there's the second law.

00:04:08.340 --> 00:04:09.757 align:middle line:84%
And the rule for
the second law is

00:04:09.757 --> 00:04:11.638 align:middle line:84%
you must obey the second
law unless it comes

00:04:11.638 --> 00:04:12.930 align:middle line:90%
in conflict with the first law.

00:04:12.930 --> 00:04:15.090 align:middle line:84%
Then you disobey the second law
and do the first law, right?

00:04:15.090 --> 00:04:16.707 align:middle line:84%
And there's a third
law, which you

00:04:16.707 --> 00:04:18.540 align:middle line:84%
should do unless it
conflicts with the first

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:19.620 align:middle line:90%
or the second law.

00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:21.390 align:middle line:90%
So this is like that, right?

00:04:21.390 --> 00:04:26.760 align:middle line:84%
So Japanese wants the accent
to be on the second word, oh,

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:28.770 align:middle line:84%
unless that's going to
make the accent final.

00:04:28.770 --> 00:04:30.840 align:middle line:90%
Then you don't do that.

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:33.480 align:middle line:90%
Does that make sense?

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:37.530 align:middle line:84%
That's a way-- or to put
that another way, don't

00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:39.150 align:middle line:84%
put the accent on
the last syllable

00:04:39.150 --> 00:04:42.355 align:middle line:84%
is more important than put
the accent on the second word.

00:04:42.355 --> 00:04:43.980 align:middle line:84%
That's the picture
that we end up with.

00:04:43.980 --> 00:04:50.820 align:middle line:84%
So you get "kagaWAken" with the
accent at the end of "kagawa"

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:53.280 align:middle line:90%
and not "kagawaKEN."

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:57.810 align:middle line:90%
You don't put accent at the end.

00:04:57.810 --> 00:04:59.730 align:middle line:84%
There's a standard
way of representing

00:04:59.730 --> 00:05:02.400 align:middle line:84%
these kinds of facts, these
bodies of facts, which

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:04.650 align:middle line:84%
involves this table
that I've got here

00:05:04.650 --> 00:05:07.080 align:middle line:90%
at the bottom of the slide.

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:09.930 align:middle line:84%
This was an approach
to phonology that was

00:05:09.930 --> 00:05:13.860 align:middle line:90%
invented by people who--

00:05:13.860 --> 00:05:15.960 align:middle line:84%
one of them in
particular, Alan Prince,

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:18.360 align:middle line:90%
is a brilliant, brilliant man.

00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:20.380 align:middle line:84%
But he enjoys
intimidating people.

00:05:20.380 --> 00:05:23.100 align:middle line:84%
And so instead of
calling this a table,

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:25.380 align:middle line:90%
he decided to call it a tableau.

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:28.410 align:middle line:84%
So he gives it a
French name in order

00:05:28.410 --> 00:05:31.050 align:middle line:84%
to be as intimidating
as possible.

00:05:31.050 --> 00:05:35.940 align:middle line:84%
So I'll adopt that because
that's what phonologists,

00:05:35.940 --> 00:05:38.620 align:middle line:84%
linguists standardly
call these objects.

00:05:38.620 --> 00:05:40.470 align:middle line:90%
This is a tableau.

00:05:40.470 --> 00:05:43.680 align:middle line:84%
And what the tableau
represents is this interplay

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:46.350 align:middle line:84%
between the different
laws that are governing

00:05:46.350 --> 00:05:48.510 align:middle line:90%
where the accent goes.

00:05:48.510 --> 00:05:52.560 align:middle line:84%
The idea is the most important
laws are toward the left.

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:56.130 align:middle line:84%
And the least important
laws are toward the right.

00:05:56.130 --> 00:05:58.950 align:middle line:84%
And what you do is
imagine all of the places

00:05:58.950 --> 00:06:00.420 align:middle line:90%
where you could put the accent.

00:06:00.420 --> 00:06:05.160 align:middle line:84%
And you ask yourself, how does
that placement of the accent

00:06:05.160 --> 00:06:08.040 align:middle line:84%
square with the
various different laws?

00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:12.750 align:middle line:84%
The little asterisks mark places
where one of the candidates,

00:06:12.750 --> 00:06:15.870 align:middle line:84%
one of the options
you're considering,

00:06:15.870 --> 00:06:18.040 align:middle line:90%
violates one of the laws.

00:06:18.040 --> 00:06:23.130 align:middle line:84%
So for example, the first one,
which kept both of the accents

00:06:23.130 --> 00:06:27.420 align:middle line:84%
in the parts of the compound,
just kept them both,

00:06:27.420 --> 00:06:29.160 align:middle line:84%
violates the most
important law, which is

00:06:29.160 --> 00:06:30.540 align:middle line:90%
you may only have one accent.

00:06:30.540 --> 00:06:32.920 align:middle line:90%
So it gets a star right there.

00:06:32.920 --> 00:06:35.040 align:middle line:84%
And that eliminates that
one from competition

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:38.700 align:middle line:84%
because it's violated the most
important law, the one that's

00:06:38.700 --> 00:06:40.840 align:middle line:90%
furthest on the left.

00:06:40.840 --> 00:06:44.400 align:middle line:84%
And then what I've done
is consider examples

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:46.830 align:middle line:84%
where the accent is-- it's a
four-syllable word, so we're

00:06:46.830 --> 00:06:50.040 align:middle line:84%
considering putting the single
accent on any of the four

00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:51.000 align:middle line:90%
syllables.

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:54.600 align:middle line:84%
The first two, which
have the accent

00:06:54.600 --> 00:06:56.100 align:middle line:84%
on the first or the
second syllable,

00:06:56.100 --> 00:06:57.530 align:middle line:84%
violate the second
law, which says

00:06:57.530 --> 00:06:59.530 align:middle line:84%
put the accent near the
boundary between the two

00:06:59.530 --> 00:07:00.730 align:middle line:90%
halves of the compound.

00:07:00.730 --> 00:07:04.350 align:middle line:84%
So those are ones in
which the accent is not

00:07:04.350 --> 00:07:07.630 align:middle line:84%
on a syllable that's
next to the boundary.

00:07:07.630 --> 00:07:13.140 align:middle line:84%
And then the next to last one,
which is the one that wins--

00:07:13.140 --> 00:07:15.632 align:middle line:84%
that's the one that obeys
all of the conditions

00:07:15.632 --> 00:07:17.340 align:middle line:84%
except for the least
important one, which

00:07:17.340 --> 00:07:21.300 align:middle line:84%
is it's best to put your
accent on the last word, right?

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:23.910 align:middle line:90%


00:07:23.910 --> 00:07:26.220 align:middle line:84%
You notice, there is
no perfect candidate.

00:07:26.220 --> 00:07:29.040 align:middle line:84%
There isn't anything that
doesn't have any stars.

00:07:29.040 --> 00:07:30.630 align:middle line:84%
What the tableau
does is allow us

00:07:30.630 --> 00:07:35.730 align:middle line:84%
to think in a
formal way about how

00:07:35.730 --> 00:07:39.218 align:middle line:84%
you satisfy conditions that are
not compatible with each other.

00:07:39.218 --> 00:07:41.010 align:middle line:84%
And I just realized I
get to take this off.

00:07:41.010 --> 00:07:43.680 align:middle line:90%
I keep forgetting that.

00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:45.100 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:07:45.100 --> 00:07:47.770 align:middle line:84%
So that's what the tableau
is meant to represent.

00:07:47.770 --> 00:07:50.080 align:middle line:84%
Are there are questions
about the tableau?

00:07:50.080 --> 00:07:52.750 align:middle line:84%
There's a standard way
to represent the fact

00:07:52.750 --> 00:07:55.337 align:middle line:84%
that the winner is
that next to last one

00:07:55.337 --> 00:07:57.670 align:middle line:84%
there, where the stress is
on the next to last syllable,

00:07:57.670 --> 00:07:59.462 align:middle line:84%
the accent is on the
next to last syllable,

00:07:59.462 --> 00:08:02.240 align:middle line:84%
which is to put a pointing
finger next to it like that.

00:08:02.240 --> 00:08:04.780 align:middle line:90%
This is the one that wins.

00:08:04.780 --> 00:08:08.980 align:middle line:84%
Optimality theory-- this
approach to phonology

00:08:08.980 --> 00:08:10.520 align:middle line:90%
is called optimality theory.

00:08:10.520 --> 00:08:12.430 align:middle line:90%
And I don't know.

00:08:12.430 --> 00:08:14.080 align:middle line:84%
I guess they just
liked the font that

00:08:14.080 --> 00:08:15.330 align:middle line:90%
had the pointing finger in it.

00:08:15.330 --> 00:08:17.594 align:middle line:90%
So they decided to use it.

00:08:17.594 --> 00:08:18.137 align:middle line:90%
OK?

00:08:18.137 --> 00:08:18.970 align:middle line:90%
This is an approach.

00:08:18.970 --> 00:08:19.906 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:08:19.906 --> 00:08:24.710 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Question about--
there is an accent on--

00:08:24.710 --> 00:08:27.570 align:middle line:84%
how do we know there's
an accent on "ken"

00:08:27.570 --> 00:08:30.593 align:middle line:84%
if it just never shows up
because every time [INAUDIBLE]??

00:08:30.593 --> 00:08:32.510 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Oh, you
mean why am I putting

00:08:32.510 --> 00:08:34.429 align:middle line:90%
an accent on "ken" over there?

00:08:34.429 --> 00:08:36.770 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Because every
time "ken" shows up,

00:08:36.770 --> 00:08:39.000 align:middle line:90%
one syllable is at the end.

00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:42.130 align:middle line:84%
So there's no-- so it's
always the final accent.

00:08:42.130 --> 00:08:43.630 align:middle line:84%
So how do we know
there's an accent?

00:08:43.630 --> 00:08:46.190 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah,
that's a very good point.

00:08:46.190 --> 00:08:48.790 align:middle line:84%
It is possible to
pronounce "ken" by itself,

00:08:48.790 --> 00:08:50.720 align:middle line:90%
not as part of a compound.

00:08:50.720 --> 00:08:53.590 align:middle line:84%
So if you want to talk about
"the prefecture," it's a word.

00:08:53.590 --> 00:08:56.770 align:middle line:84%
It means "prefecture,"
which is like a state.

00:08:56.770 --> 00:09:00.310 align:middle line:84%
It's an administrative
subdivision of Japan.

00:09:00.310 --> 00:09:02.303 align:middle line:84%
And it's possible to
say that word by itself.

00:09:02.303 --> 00:09:03.220 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: So unbounded.

00:09:03.220 --> 00:09:04.178 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Sorry?

00:09:04.178 --> 00:09:05.850 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: So it's unfounded?

00:09:05.850 --> 00:09:08.020 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: It's?

00:09:08.020 --> 00:09:09.020 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: It's unbounded?

00:09:09.020 --> 00:09:11.300 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
"ken" is unbounded?

00:09:11.300 --> 00:09:13.530 align:middle line:84%
I don't think I
know what you mean.

00:09:13.530 --> 00:09:14.780 align:middle line:90%
Oh, it's not a bound morpheme.

00:09:14.780 --> 00:09:15.080 align:middle line:90%
That's right.

00:09:15.080 --> 00:09:16.220 align:middle line:90%
It can be a free morpheme.

00:09:16.220 --> 00:09:16.938 align:middle line:90%
Yes, yes.

00:09:16.938 --> 00:09:17.480 align:middle line:90%
That's right.

00:09:17.480 --> 00:09:21.500 align:middle line:84%
So you can say in Japanese,
"The prefecture is large"

00:09:21.500 --> 00:09:22.740 align:middle line:90%
or whatever.

00:09:22.740 --> 00:09:27.160 align:middle line:84%
And then you can show
that it's accented, yeah.

00:09:27.160 --> 00:09:28.100 align:middle line:90%
Good question.

00:09:28.100 --> 00:09:31.700 align:middle line:84%
There are other questions
people have about this?

00:09:31.700 --> 00:09:32.690 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:09:32.690 --> 00:09:36.170 align:middle line:84%
So this is just meant to
illustrate the workings

00:09:36.170 --> 00:09:38.840 align:middle line:90%
of optimality theory.

00:09:38.840 --> 00:09:40.870 align:middle line:90%
Why am I showing you this one?

00:09:40.870 --> 00:09:41.780 align:middle line:90%
I'm not sure.

00:09:41.780 --> 00:09:43.050 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:09:43.050 --> 00:09:44.010 align:middle line:90%
Oh, I see.

00:09:44.010 --> 00:09:47.490 align:middle line:84%
This is a similar
tableau for "French fry."

00:09:47.490 --> 00:09:51.480 align:middle line:84%
So this has the
same constraints,

00:09:51.480 --> 00:09:55.530 align:middle line:84%
these things that I put
in all caps up at the top.

00:09:55.530 --> 00:09:59.070 align:middle line:84%
In this case, you do
end up with accent

00:09:59.070 --> 00:10:01.650 align:middle line:84%
at the beginning of
potato on the second word.

00:10:01.650 --> 00:10:03.610 align:middle line:84%
That actually is a
perfect candidate.

00:10:03.610 --> 00:10:06.990 align:middle line:84%
So this is an example in
which none of the laws

00:10:06.990 --> 00:10:09.757 align:middle line:84%
are violated by
the best candidate.

00:10:09.757 --> 00:10:11.590 align:middle line:84%
The laws aren't in
conflict with each other.

00:10:11.590 --> 00:10:13.173 align:middle line:84%
So you can put accent
at the beginning

00:10:13.173 --> 00:10:15.450 align:middle line:90%
of the last word, "potato."

00:10:15.450 --> 00:10:17.160 align:middle line:90%
And it won't be final.

00:10:17.160 --> 00:10:18.930 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, yeah?

00:10:18.930 --> 00:10:22.224 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: So for example, if this
perfect candidate didn't exist,

00:10:22.224 --> 00:10:24.182 align:middle line:84%
and you were having to
choose between the first

00:10:24.182 --> 00:10:24.930 align:middle line:90%
and the third--

00:10:24.930 --> 00:10:25.290 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:10:25.290 --> 00:10:26.748 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: --you
would want to choose

00:10:26.748 --> 00:10:29.392 align:middle line:84%
the third because it violates
a rule that's less important.

00:10:29.392 --> 00:10:31.350 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah,
that's absolutely right.

00:10:31.350 --> 00:10:33.870 align:middle line:84%
So if, for some reason,
that fourth candidate

00:10:33.870 --> 00:10:36.390 align:middle line:84%
were impossible-- say,
there was another very high

00:10:36.390 --> 00:10:43.980 align:middle line:84%
ranking constraint-- don't put
accent on "potato," then yes.

00:10:43.980 --> 00:10:45.240 align:middle line:90%
The third would win.

00:10:45.240 --> 00:10:45.990 align:middle line:90%
That's right.

00:10:45.990 --> 00:10:48.508 align:middle line:84%
And actually, we can
see that by looking

00:10:48.508 --> 00:10:49.800 align:middle line:90%
at the behavior of "kagaWAken."

00:10:49.800 --> 00:10:53.880 align:middle line:84%
So if we eliminate the
option of putting accent

00:10:53.880 --> 00:10:56.400 align:middle line:84%
on the last word, that's
where the accent goes.

00:10:56.400 --> 00:10:58.630 align:middle line:84%
It goes at the end
of the first word.

00:10:58.630 --> 00:11:01.950 align:middle line:84%
So it's the best
you can do, right?

00:11:01.950 --> 00:11:05.957 align:middle line:84%
Still near the boundary,
still only one accent, just

00:11:05.957 --> 00:11:07.540 align:middle line:84%
didn't manage to
accent the last word,

00:11:07.540 --> 00:11:08.832 align:middle line:90%
which is what you'd like to do.

00:11:08.832 --> 00:11:11.880 align:middle line:84%
But it's not high on
your list of constraints.

00:11:11.880 --> 00:11:12.735 align:middle line:90%
Yep.

00:11:12.735 --> 00:11:14.410 align:middle line:90%
Does that make sense?

00:11:14.410 --> 00:11:18.510 align:middle line:84%
So this is an approach to
thinking about phonology.

00:11:18.510 --> 00:11:21.190 align:middle line:84%
Optimality theory
is the name for it.

00:11:21.190 --> 00:11:24.090 align:middle line:84%
It says-- in a way, it's
a comforting theory--

00:11:24.090 --> 00:11:25.690 align:middle line:90%
It says, "Do the best you can."

00:11:25.690 --> 00:11:27.108 align:middle line:90%
You have all of these things.

00:11:27.108 --> 00:11:28.650 align:middle line:84%
Probably, all of
you have encountered

00:11:28.650 --> 00:11:30.330 align:middle line:84%
things like this
in other domains

00:11:30.330 --> 00:11:32.580 align:middle line:84%
at MIT if you've ever dealt
with engineering problems.

00:11:32.580 --> 00:11:35.588 align:middle line:84%
There are probably cases
where you have various things

00:11:35.588 --> 00:11:36.630 align:middle line:90%
that you want to achieve.

00:11:36.630 --> 00:11:39.090 align:middle line:84%
And you can't
optimize all of them.

00:11:39.090 --> 00:11:42.130 align:middle line:84%
You have to decide which
ones are the most important.

00:11:42.130 --> 00:11:44.460 align:middle line:84%
And the idea here is that
language is like that.

00:11:44.460 --> 00:11:47.010 align:middle line:84%
There are cases where
you have to decide

00:11:47.010 --> 00:11:50.850 align:middle line:84%
among your various
desiderata which

00:11:50.850 --> 00:11:52.950 align:middle line:90%
ones you care about the most.

00:11:52.950 --> 00:11:55.170 align:middle line:84%
The hope in optimality
theory is that we'll

00:11:55.170 --> 00:12:00.150 align:middle line:84%
end up with a list of these
constraints, these laws,

00:12:00.150 --> 00:12:02.920 align:middle line:84%
these rules that
will be universal.

00:12:02.920 --> 00:12:05.790 align:middle line:84%
And the differences
between languages

00:12:05.790 --> 00:12:07.860 align:middle line:84%
are just a matter
of deciding what

00:12:07.860 --> 00:12:10.410 align:middle line:84%
order the different
constraints are in.

00:12:10.410 --> 00:12:16.770 align:middle line:84%
So there could be languages
in which the one accent

00:12:16.770 --> 00:12:21.840 align:middle line:84%
rule is very low ranked and
the first candidate maybe wins,

00:12:21.840 --> 00:12:22.340 align:middle line:90%
right?

00:12:22.340 --> 00:12:24.630 align:middle line:84%
You could imagine a
language like that.

00:12:24.630 --> 00:12:26.800 align:middle line:84%
Tokyo Japanese
doesn't work that way.

00:12:26.800 --> 00:12:29.040 align:middle line:84%
So differences between
languages are all a matter

00:12:29.040 --> 00:12:30.900 align:middle line:90%
of reranking the constraints.

00:12:30.900 --> 00:12:34.170 align:middle line:84%
That's the mission statement
of optimality theory,

00:12:34.170 --> 00:12:36.970 align:middle line:84%
is to try to figure out how
the world could work that way.

00:12:36.970 --> 00:12:37.470 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:12:37.470 --> 00:12:39.905 align:middle line:90%


00:12:39.905 --> 00:12:40.405 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:12:40.405 --> 00:12:43.130 align:middle line:90%


00:12:43.130 --> 00:12:45.080 align:middle line:84%
Optimality theory
was partly inspired

00:12:45.080 --> 00:12:48.013 align:middle line:84%
by the discovery of these
kinds of conspiracies

00:12:48.013 --> 00:12:50.180 align:middle line:84%
that we started talking
about when I was showing you

00:12:50.180 --> 00:12:51.500 align:middle line:90%
some stuff about Yawelmani.

00:12:51.500 --> 00:12:54.560 align:middle line:84%
And now we've seen another
example from Japanese.

00:12:54.560 --> 00:12:57.320 align:middle line:84%
For Yawelmani, the
conspiracy was,

00:12:57.320 --> 00:12:59.540 align:middle line:84%
I don't want three
consonants in a row.

00:12:59.540 --> 00:13:02.210 align:middle line:84%
And I'll do various
things to achieve that.

00:13:02.210 --> 00:13:05.180 align:middle line:84%
We said you could state rules
governing the various things.

00:13:05.180 --> 00:13:07.410 align:middle line:84%
And maybe in a
sense, you should.

00:13:07.410 --> 00:13:10.483 align:middle line:84%
But you-- the idea is, let's
try to keep sight of the fact

00:13:10.483 --> 00:13:11.900 align:middle line:84%
that all of these
rules are trying

00:13:11.900 --> 00:13:13.520 align:middle line:90%
to achieve the same thing.

00:13:13.520 --> 00:13:16.190 align:middle line:84%
We want to also capture
that kind of a fact.

00:13:16.190 --> 00:13:19.220 align:middle line:84%
And there's something similar
going on here in Japanese.

00:13:19.220 --> 00:13:22.310 align:middle line:84%
One of the rules, the
highest ranking rule,

00:13:22.310 --> 00:13:26.750 align:middle line:84%
was "Don't have more than
one accent in a word."

00:13:26.750 --> 00:13:31.190 align:middle line:84%
And what we saw last time was
that there are various things

00:13:31.190 --> 00:13:33.860 align:middle line:84%
that Japanese does
to achieve this

00:13:33.860 --> 00:13:36.150 align:middle line:90%
depending on the situation.

00:13:36.150 --> 00:13:41.540 align:middle line:84%
So there are words
like "gurai," which

00:13:41.540 --> 00:13:45.530 align:middle line:84%
I think I translated last time
as even, which are accented.

00:13:45.530 --> 00:13:47.540 align:middle line:84%
And if you attach them
to another word that's

00:13:47.540 --> 00:13:50.373 align:middle line:84%
also accented, you delete
the accent on the first word,

00:13:50.373 --> 00:13:52.040 align:middle line:84%
in the word you're
attaching "gurai" to.

00:13:52.040 --> 00:13:54.755 align:middle line:84%
And there are other words,
like "made," which means "to,"

00:13:54.755 --> 00:13:58.850 align:middle line:84%
or "up to," "as far as,"
which are accented but only

00:13:58.850 --> 00:14:02.720 align:middle line:84%
if they are attached
to unaccented words.

00:14:02.720 --> 00:14:05.810 align:middle line:84%
And then we've done all this
talking about compounds.

00:14:05.810 --> 00:14:08.360 align:middle line:84%
These are all different
kinds of examples in which

00:14:08.360 --> 00:14:09.380 align:middle line:90%
different things happen.

00:14:09.380 --> 00:14:10.963 align:middle line:84%
There are different
strategies, right?

00:14:10.963 --> 00:14:13.880 align:middle line:84%
So for "gurai," it's "Delete
the accent on the word

00:14:13.880 --> 00:14:15.680 align:middle line:90%
you've attached 'gurai' to."

00:14:15.680 --> 00:14:17.570 align:middle line:84%
For "made," it's
"Delete the accent on

00:14:17.570 --> 00:14:19.070 align:middle line:90%
'made' for the compounds."

00:14:19.070 --> 00:14:21.233 align:middle line:84%
We've now talked
about this computation

00:14:21.233 --> 00:14:23.150 align:middle line:84%
that you go through to
try to figure out where

00:14:23.150 --> 00:14:24.710 align:middle line:90%
the single accent should go.

00:14:24.710 --> 00:14:26.300 align:middle line:84%
But what all of these
things are doing

00:14:26.300 --> 00:14:29.360 align:middle line:84%
is trying to arrange for there
to just be a single accent.

00:14:29.360 --> 00:14:31.220 align:middle line:84%
And one of the virtues
of optimality theory

00:14:31.220 --> 00:14:36.290 align:middle line:84%
is that it allows us to make
that part of our analysis.

00:14:36.290 --> 00:14:38.930 align:middle line:84%
If we just stated
these rules, we'd

00:14:38.930 --> 00:14:41.210 align:middle line:84%
be losing sight of the
fact that these rules all

00:14:41.210 --> 00:14:43.468 align:middle line:84%
seem to be trying to
achieve the same thing.

00:14:43.468 --> 00:14:46.010 align:middle line:84%
That's one of the things people
who work on optimality theory

00:14:46.010 --> 00:14:46.940 align:middle line:90%
are trying to get.

00:14:46.940 --> 00:14:48.260 align:middle line:84%
And then of course,
the goal is to try

00:14:48.260 --> 00:14:49.850 align:middle line:84%
to understand why we
have the constraints

00:14:49.850 --> 00:14:51.110 align:middle line:90%
that we have and not others.

00:14:51.110 --> 00:14:53.690 align:middle line:84%
That's another thing people
who work on this try to do.

00:14:53.690 --> 00:14:59.290 align:middle line:90%


00:14:59.290 --> 00:14:59.790 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:14:59.790 --> 00:15:02.290 align:middle line:90%
I just said that and that.

00:15:02.290 --> 00:15:05.790 align:middle line:84%
So remember, Yawelmani, which
was "Avoid strings of three

00:15:05.790 --> 00:15:09.210 align:middle line:84%
consonants either by inserting
vowels or by deleting

00:15:09.210 --> 00:15:12.330 align:middle line:90%
'h' if you have an 'h'."

00:15:12.330 --> 00:15:18.360 align:middle line:84%
So I've been saying we could
just state these rules.

00:15:18.360 --> 00:15:20.340 align:middle line:84%
But we'd be missing
a generalization.

00:15:20.340 --> 00:15:22.770 align:middle line:84%
Yawelmani is trying to avoid
three consonants in a row.

00:15:22.770 --> 00:15:27.360 align:middle line:84%
Japanese is trying to avoid
two accents in a single word.

00:15:27.360 --> 00:15:28.230 align:middle line:90%
Maybe it's obvious.

00:15:28.230 --> 00:15:30.520 align:middle line:84%
We don't want to miss
generalizations like that.

00:15:30.520 --> 00:15:34.860 align:middle line:84%
We want to find them
and understand them.

00:15:34.860 --> 00:15:38.880 align:middle line:84%
So similar kind of thing that
we could think about here--

00:15:38.880 --> 00:15:41.610 align:middle line:84%
we've talked now a couple
of times about Polish.

00:15:41.610 --> 00:15:43.530 align:middle line:84%
Polish is one of the
number of languages

00:15:43.530 --> 00:15:47.040 align:middle line:84%
in which if you have
certain kinds of consonants

00:15:47.040 --> 00:15:49.350 align:middle line:84%
at the ends of words,
consonants like "b,"

00:15:49.350 --> 00:15:51.750 align:middle line:84%
and "d," and "g," and
"z," we convince ourselves

00:15:51.750 --> 00:15:52.560 align:middle line:90%
that they devoice.

00:15:52.560 --> 00:15:55.170 align:middle line:84%
They become voiceless
at the ends of words.

00:15:55.170 --> 00:15:57.780 align:middle line:84%
That allows us to capture
various kinds of facts

00:15:57.780 --> 00:16:00.720 align:middle line:84%
about what happens to Polish
words when they become plural--

00:16:00.720 --> 00:16:06.000 align:middle line:84%
namely, that consonants that
sound voiceless when they're

00:16:06.000 --> 00:16:09.240 align:middle line:84%
at the ends of words
become voiced sometimes

00:16:09.240 --> 00:16:11.640 align:middle line:84%
when they're no longer at
the end of words, we decided.

00:16:11.640 --> 00:16:14.280 align:middle line:84%
That's because they're
underlyingly voiced.

00:16:14.280 --> 00:16:18.730 align:middle line:84%
And they devoice when
they're at the ends of words.

00:16:18.730 --> 00:16:20.280 align:middle line:90%
So this is all familiar.

00:16:20.280 --> 00:16:21.745 align:middle line:90%
You all remember Polish?

00:16:21.745 --> 00:16:22.620 align:middle line:90%
It's not just Polish.

00:16:22.620 --> 00:16:26.350 align:middle line:84%
There are lots of
languages that do this.

00:16:26.350 --> 00:16:30.030 align:middle line:84%
And so what I'm inviting you
to do at the end of this slide

00:16:30.030 --> 00:16:33.180 align:middle line:84%
is to imagine two kinds of
stories about Polish-- one

00:16:33.180 --> 00:16:36.360 align:middle line:84%
that would say, yeah, final
consonants devoice in Polish,

00:16:36.360 --> 00:16:38.370 align:middle line:84%
and another that
would say, well, there

00:16:38.370 --> 00:16:39.480 align:middle line:90%
are four rules, actually.

00:16:39.480 --> 00:16:42.210 align:middle line:84%
There's a rule that says
"b" voices when it's final,

00:16:42.210 --> 00:16:44.520 align:middle line:84%
and a rule that says "d"
devoices when it's final,

00:16:44.520 --> 00:16:47.790 align:middle line:84%
and a rule that says "g"
devoices when it's-- yeah?

00:16:47.790 --> 00:16:50.640 align:middle line:84%
Both of those would capture
all of the Polish facts.

00:16:50.640 --> 00:16:53.230 align:middle line:90%
One of them is clearly better.

00:16:53.230 --> 00:16:57.660 align:middle line:84%
So we want to understand why
we get the particular phenomena

00:16:57.660 --> 00:16:58.890 align:middle line:90%
that we do in Polish.

00:16:58.890 --> 00:17:01.470 align:middle line:84%
Is it clear to everybody
that it would be better

00:17:01.470 --> 00:17:03.310 align:middle line:90%
to do things that way?

00:17:03.310 --> 00:17:07.630 align:middle line:84%
And what we're doing
here is similar.

00:17:07.630 --> 00:17:10.920 align:middle line:90%
Why would it be better?

00:17:10.920 --> 00:17:12.230 align:middle line:90%
What would be wrong--

00:17:12.230 --> 00:17:15.599 align:middle line:84%
why is it better to be able to
say final consonants devoice

00:17:15.599 --> 00:17:19.900 align:middle line:84%
than to say, well, we
have these four rules.

00:17:19.900 --> 00:17:20.490 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, Faith?

00:17:20.490 --> 00:17:21.660 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: I think
you're addressing

00:17:21.660 --> 00:17:22.948 align:middle line:90%
the more general phenomenon.

00:17:22.948 --> 00:17:25.852 align:middle line:84%
It's very difficult to
catalog every single letter

00:17:25.852 --> 00:17:29.724 align:middle line:84%
and to have vowel sound
as something like that

00:17:29.724 --> 00:17:32.053 align:middle line:84%
because you eventually have
a list that's very long.

00:17:32.053 --> 00:17:32.970 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:17:32.970 --> 00:17:34.410 align:middle line:84%
So it might make
it easier to make

00:17:34.410 --> 00:17:35.970 align:middle line:90%
cross-linguistic comparisons.

00:17:35.970 --> 00:17:37.350 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, that's a very good point.

00:17:37.350 --> 00:17:39.930 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, yeah.

00:17:39.930 --> 00:17:40.430 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:17:40.430 --> 00:17:43.260 align:middle line:90%


00:17:43.260 --> 00:17:45.285 align:middle line:90%
We also maybe--

00:17:45.285 --> 00:17:47.160 align:middle line:84%
I don't know if this is
a good way to say it.

00:17:47.160 --> 00:17:49.030 align:middle line:90%
Maybe it is.

00:17:49.030 --> 00:17:53.650 align:middle line:84%
We want to understand why
the sounds that become

00:17:53.650 --> 00:17:56.845 align:middle line:84%
voiceless at the ends of
words in Polish are "b,"

00:17:56.845 --> 00:18:04.690 align:middle line:84%
"d," "g," and "z," and not
"b," "d," "a," and "o," right?

00:18:04.690 --> 00:18:08.470 align:middle line:84%
If we're just making four
rules for those four letters,

00:18:08.470 --> 00:18:11.590 align:middle line:90%
then it's not clear.

00:18:11.590 --> 00:18:14.320 align:middle line:84%
There's no reason why
one of those rules

00:18:14.320 --> 00:18:15.740 align:middle line:90%
is more natural than the other.

00:18:15.740 --> 00:18:17.620 align:middle line:90%
But as I keep saying--

00:18:17.620 --> 00:18:20.270 align:middle line:84%
what's happening here in
Polish is extremely common.

00:18:20.270 --> 00:18:22.283 align:middle line:90%
So you get it in Turkish.

00:18:22.283 --> 00:18:23.200 align:middle line:90%
You get it in Russian.

00:18:23.200 --> 00:18:25.810 align:middle line:84%
You get it in a bunch
of different languages--

00:18:25.810 --> 00:18:27.700 align:middle line:90%
German.

00:18:27.700 --> 00:18:31.512 align:middle line:84%
And so we want to
understand why it's common.

00:18:31.512 --> 00:18:33.220 align:middle line:84%
And part of understanding
why it's common

00:18:33.220 --> 00:18:36.880 align:middle line:84%
is being able to say that that's
a common rule, a standard rule

00:18:36.880 --> 00:18:38.540 align:middle line:90%
that we see all over the place.

00:18:38.540 --> 00:18:41.080 align:middle line:84%
And then we can get to
work on understanding it.

00:18:41.080 --> 00:18:42.520 align:middle line:84%
If we just have
these four rules,

00:18:42.520 --> 00:18:44.860 align:middle line:84%
then it's unclear why those
four rules are so common,

00:18:44.860 --> 00:18:49.720 align:middle line:84%
whereas "b," "d," "a," and "o"
devoice at the ends of words

00:18:49.720 --> 00:18:50.837 align:middle line:90%
is not at all common.

00:18:50.837 --> 00:18:52.420 align:middle line:84%
I don't know of any
languages that do.

00:18:52.420 --> 00:18:54.130 align:middle line:90%
Does that make sense?

00:18:54.130 --> 00:18:57.730 align:middle line:84%
That's another sense in which
making generalizations is good.

00:18:57.730 --> 00:19:00.430 align:middle line:84%
This is becoming a philosophy
of science question, yeah.

00:19:00.430 --> 00:19:02.560 align:middle line:90%
It's like, why is science good?

00:19:02.560 --> 00:19:04.785 align:middle line:84%
Why is it good to
make generalizations?

00:19:04.785 --> 00:19:06.160 align:middle line:84%
Because it helps
us to understand

00:19:06.160 --> 00:19:09.075 align:middle line:84%
the world better, for Faith's
reason, as well as the reason

00:19:09.075 --> 00:19:09.700 align:middle line:90%
I just offered.

00:19:09.700 --> 00:19:10.200 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:19:10.200 --> 00:19:14.368 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Is the vowel still
a vowel when you devoice it?

00:19:14.368 --> 00:19:15.910 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
Yeah, yes, arguably.

00:19:15.910 --> 00:19:17.830 align:middle line:84%
So there are languages
that devoice vowels.

00:19:17.830 --> 00:19:20.800 align:middle line:90%


00:19:20.800 --> 00:19:22.660 align:middle line:90%
Mokilese is one.

00:19:22.660 --> 00:19:24.520 align:middle line:90%
Oh, Japanese is another.

00:19:24.520 --> 00:19:31.690 align:middle line:84%
So Japanese has a general
rule devoicing its high vowels

00:19:31.690 --> 00:19:36.550 align:middle line:84%
when they are between
voiceless consonants, I guess.

00:19:36.550 --> 00:19:41.260 align:middle line:84%
So here's the Japanese
word for "like."

00:19:41.260 --> 00:19:43.990 align:middle line:84%
And it's pronounced
"suki," "suki."

00:19:43.990 --> 00:19:45.640 align:middle line:90%
So the oo isn't pronounced.

00:19:45.640 --> 00:19:48.820 align:middle line:84%
You don't say "soo-kee"
unless you're being very slow

00:19:48.820 --> 00:19:50.560 align:middle line:90%
and careful.

00:19:50.560 --> 00:19:53.738 align:middle line:84%
And it's still a
vowel in the sense

00:19:53.738 --> 00:19:55.780 align:middle line:84%
that your tongue and the
rest of your vocal tract

00:19:55.780 --> 00:19:57.010 align:middle line:84%
is still in the
configuration that it

00:19:57.010 --> 00:19:58.930 align:middle line:84%
would be if your vocal
chords were vibrating.

00:19:58.930 --> 00:20:01.180 align:middle line:90%
But they aren't.

00:20:01.180 --> 00:20:04.060 align:middle line:84%
So yeah, there arguably
are voiceless vowels.

00:20:04.060 --> 00:20:04.660 align:middle line:90%
Yes, Joseph?

00:20:04.660 --> 00:20:06.910 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: What if you were
putting an affix after that,

00:20:06.910 --> 00:20:08.380 align:middle line:90%
which is "i" is high, too?

00:20:08.380 --> 00:20:09.940 align:middle line:90%
So what would happen?

00:20:09.940 --> 00:20:11.080 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Oh.

00:20:11.080 --> 00:20:12.700 align:middle line:84%
So in this particular
example, there

00:20:12.700 --> 00:20:17.800 align:middle line:84%
aren't any affixes you can add
to "suki" to devoice the vowel.

00:20:17.800 --> 00:20:23.620 align:middle line:90%
But let me think of an example.

00:20:23.620 --> 00:20:26.620 align:middle line:90%


00:20:26.620 --> 00:20:27.120 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:20:27.120 --> 00:20:35.133 align:middle line:84%
So "tatsu"-- so
this is not high.

00:20:35.133 --> 00:20:36.050 align:middle line:90%
So it doesn't devoice.

00:20:36.050 --> 00:20:39.140 align:middle line:90%
This is the verb "to stand."

00:20:39.140 --> 00:20:43.500 align:middle line:84%
The more polite
version of this--

00:20:43.500 --> 00:20:44.200 align:middle line:90%
no, no, no, no.

00:20:44.200 --> 00:20:47.033 align:middle line:90%
That's not what I want to do.

00:20:47.033 --> 00:20:48.450 align:middle line:84%
Let me try to think
of an example.

00:20:48.450 --> 00:20:50.033 align:middle line:84%
Let me try to think
of an example that

00:20:50.033 --> 00:20:51.180 align:middle line:90%
does what you want.

00:20:51.180 --> 00:20:54.510 align:middle line:84%
But there aren't any examples
that will work with "suki."

00:20:54.510 --> 00:20:57.390 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:20:57.390 --> 00:20:59.505 align:middle line:84%
OK, so when I think
of an example,

00:20:59.505 --> 00:21:01.380 align:middle line:84%
at 3:00 in the morning,
I'll give you a call.

00:21:01.380 --> 00:21:05.430 align:middle line:84%
Leave your phone number
and think of that.

00:21:05.430 --> 00:21:07.570 align:middle line:84%
There's got to be a
straightforward way to do this.

00:21:07.570 --> 00:21:10.080 align:middle line:84%
Hmm, this is going
to bother me now.

00:21:10.080 --> 00:21:10.875 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:21:10.875 --> 00:21:12.000 align:middle line:90%
Other questions about this?

00:21:12.000 --> 00:21:16.175 align:middle line:90%


00:21:16.175 --> 00:21:17.550 align:middle line:84%
Try to think of
questions while I

00:21:17.550 --> 00:21:20.460 align:middle line:84%
try to think of examples
that do what Joseph wants.

00:21:20.460 --> 00:21:24.030 align:middle line:84%
Because the past tense suffix
in Japanese-- oh, oh, oh.

00:21:24.030 --> 00:21:25.260 align:middle line:90%
[JAPANESE] oh, yeah, OK.

00:21:25.260 --> 00:21:25.770 align:middle line:90%
Here's one.

00:21:25.770 --> 00:21:28.650 align:middle line:90%


00:21:28.650 --> 00:21:31.980 align:middle line:90%
Adjectives in Japanese.

00:21:31.980 --> 00:21:37.440 align:middle line:84%
So this is the word
for new, "atarashii."

00:21:37.440 --> 00:21:45.580 align:middle line:84%
And the negative of it,
"atarashiku nai" ends--

00:21:45.580 --> 00:21:49.230 align:middle line:84%
so there's a "ku" that you
add to make the negative form.

00:21:49.230 --> 00:21:53.640 align:middle line:84%
And it ends up putting this
vowel between voiceless sounds.

00:21:53.640 --> 00:21:55.080 align:middle line:90%
And then yes, it devoices.

00:21:55.080 --> 00:21:59.070 align:middle line:84%
So it goes from "atarashii"
to "atarashiku nai,"

00:21:59.070 --> 00:22:03.030 align:middle line:84%
"atarashiku nai," or the
past tense, actually.

00:22:03.030 --> 00:22:04.710 align:middle line:84%
If you want to say
something was new,

00:22:04.710 --> 00:22:06.660 align:middle line:90%
it's "atarashii" [JAPANESE].

00:22:06.660 --> 00:22:09.730 align:middle line:84%
So again, the "i"
that's after the "sh"

00:22:09.730 --> 00:22:12.390 align:middle line:84%
devoices because there's
a voiceless sound after.

00:22:12.390 --> 00:22:13.300 align:middle line:90%
Good.

00:22:13.300 --> 00:22:13.980 align:middle line:90%
Phew!

00:22:13.980 --> 00:22:16.550 align:middle line:84%
Now I won't call you
at 2:00 in the morning.

00:22:16.550 --> 00:22:18.959 align:middle line:84%
You have any
questions about this?

00:22:18.959 --> 00:22:20.380 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:22:20.380 --> 00:22:22.730 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:22:22.730 --> 00:22:29.030 align:middle line:84%
So let's go back now to
the English plural suffix

00:22:29.030 --> 00:22:33.380 align:middle line:84%
and review something that
we have talked about.

00:22:33.380 --> 00:22:35.540 align:middle line:84%
But in the light of all
of the things that we've

00:22:35.540 --> 00:22:39.300 align:middle line:84%
talked about since then, I hope
it'll become a little clearer.

00:22:39.300 --> 00:22:42.650 align:middle line:84%
So again, we're talking about
the regular English plural

00:22:42.650 --> 00:22:43.310 align:middle line:90%
suffix.

00:22:43.310 --> 00:22:46.670 align:middle line:84%
Stop thinking about "oxen"
and "fish" and "sheep."

00:22:46.670 --> 00:22:48.980 align:middle line:90%
"Cats" and "dogs" and "bushes"--

00:22:48.980 --> 00:22:51.230 align:middle line:84%
we decided the underlying
form of the plural suffix

00:22:51.230 --> 00:22:52.100 align:middle line:90%
is a "z," right?

00:22:52.100 --> 00:22:56.060 align:middle line:84%
And it undergoes various
kinds of sound changes

00:22:56.060 --> 00:22:58.220 align:middle line:90%
depending on what's before it.

00:22:58.220 --> 00:23:02.510 align:middle line:84%
So you add "z" to all three
of these underlyingly.

00:23:02.510 --> 00:23:05.240 align:middle line:84%
And then there are
generalizations like English

00:23:05.240 --> 00:23:07.520 align:middle line:84%
doesn't like words that
end in a "z" that's

00:23:07.520 --> 00:23:09.950 align:middle line:90%
preceded by a voiceless sound.

00:23:09.950 --> 00:23:15.290 align:middle line:84%
And words don't get to end in
two strident consonants, where

00:23:15.290 --> 00:23:18.260 align:middle line:84%
stridents are things
like the "sh" and the "z"

00:23:18.260 --> 00:23:22.340 align:middle line:84%
that's at the end
of "bushes," yeah?

00:23:22.340 --> 00:23:23.990 align:middle line:90%
So those are generalizations.

00:23:23.990 --> 00:23:26.065 align:middle line:84%
But we said one of
the attractive things

00:23:26.065 --> 00:23:28.190 align:middle line:84%
about this way of thinking
about the English plural

00:23:28.190 --> 00:23:29.565 align:middle line:84%
is that these are
generalizations

00:23:29.565 --> 00:23:31.760 align:middle line:84%
that just seem to hold
about English generally.

00:23:31.760 --> 00:23:33.290 align:middle line:84%
English doesn't
have any words that

00:23:33.290 --> 00:23:35.750 align:middle line:84%
end in a "z" preceded
by a voiceless sound.

00:23:35.750 --> 00:23:38.120 align:middle line:84%
And we can rely on
English phonology

00:23:38.120 --> 00:23:41.535 align:middle line:84%
to demand that you do something
if you add "z" to cat.

00:23:41.535 --> 00:23:44.480 align:middle line:90%


00:23:44.480 --> 00:23:48.020 align:middle line:84%
And again, we've
said this before.

00:23:48.020 --> 00:23:50.540 align:middle line:84%
I just want to say it again
a little more clearly.

00:23:50.540 --> 00:23:52.220 align:middle line:90%
There are these conditions.

00:23:52.220 --> 00:23:54.560 align:middle line:84%
And then there are
also these procedures

00:23:54.560 --> 00:23:56.630 align:middle line:90%
for repairing these violations.

00:23:56.630 --> 00:24:00.920 align:middle line:84%
So what do you do about the
fact that you add a "z" to cat?

00:24:00.920 --> 00:24:03.920 align:middle line:84%
Well, you devoice
the "z," right?

00:24:03.920 --> 00:24:06.470 align:middle line:84%
If the condition just says
you can't have a "z" preceded

00:24:06.470 --> 00:24:08.810 align:middle line:84%
by something voiceless,
there are various things

00:24:08.810 --> 00:24:10.790 align:middle line:84%
you could have done
to fix that problem.

00:24:10.790 --> 00:24:13.250 align:middle line:84%
You could have voiced
the "d," right?

00:24:13.250 --> 00:24:15.500 align:middle line:84%
So the plural of "cat"
could be "cads," right?

00:24:15.500 --> 00:24:18.080 align:middle line:84%
That would be a
good English word.

00:24:18.080 --> 00:24:21.470 align:middle line:84%
Or you could add a vowel like
you do in "bushes," right?

00:24:21.470 --> 00:24:23.420 align:middle line:84%
The plural of "cat"
could be "cat-es."

00:24:23.420 --> 00:24:27.140 align:middle line:84%
There are various things
that would fix that problem

00:24:27.140 --> 00:24:28.130 align:middle line:90%
if that's the problem.

00:24:28.130 --> 00:24:29.960 align:middle line:84%
But this is the
thing that you do.

00:24:29.960 --> 00:24:32.360 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, it's like when we
were talking about Yawelmani

00:24:32.360 --> 00:24:34.470 align:middle line:84%
We said you can't have
three consonants in a row.

00:24:34.470 --> 00:24:35.550 align:middle line:90%
What do you do about it?

00:24:35.550 --> 00:24:37.423 align:middle line:84%
Well, it depends on
the situation, right?

00:24:37.423 --> 00:24:39.590 align:middle line:84%
So if one of them is an
"h," you get rid of the "h."

00:24:39.590 --> 00:24:42.380 align:middle line:84%
If not, then you insert
a vowel in this position

00:24:42.380 --> 00:24:43.720 align:middle line:90%
and not that position.

00:24:43.720 --> 00:24:47.700 align:middle line:90%
It's similar thing.

00:24:47.700 --> 00:24:54.170 align:middle line:84%
So this is all the way of
saying it's worthwhile, maybe,

00:24:54.170 --> 00:24:58.670 align:middle line:84%
to distinguish between the
phonotactic considerations

00:24:58.670 --> 00:25:01.220 align:middle line:84%
or the well-formedness
considerations that tell you

00:25:01.220 --> 00:25:04.340 align:middle line:84%
this language doesn't like
three consonants in a row,

00:25:04.340 --> 00:25:06.320 align:middle line:84%
or two accented
syllables in a word,

00:25:06.320 --> 00:25:10.610 align:middle line:84%
or words ending in a "z"
preceded by a voiceless sound.

00:25:10.610 --> 00:25:12.200 align:middle line:90%
There's that condition.

00:25:12.200 --> 00:25:14.900 align:middle line:84%
And then there's how
do you repair it?

00:25:14.900 --> 00:25:17.270 align:middle line:84%
What kind of a
repair do you use?

00:25:17.270 --> 00:25:19.460 align:middle line:84%
So there's the
well-formedness condition.

00:25:19.460 --> 00:25:20.820 align:middle line:90%
And then there's the repair.

00:25:20.820 --> 00:25:22.820 align:middle line:84%
So in this particular
case, the procedures

00:25:22.820 --> 00:25:26.990 align:middle line:90%
are devoice and insert schwa.

00:25:26.990 --> 00:25:32.750 align:middle line:84%
There's a particularly striking
bit of evidence for this way

00:25:32.750 --> 00:25:36.080 align:middle line:84%
of dividing things
up, this distinction

00:25:36.080 --> 00:25:38.870 align:middle line:84%
that I'm drawing between
well-formedness conditions

00:25:38.870 --> 00:25:43.400 align:middle line:84%
on words, on the one hand,
and types of possible repair

00:25:43.400 --> 00:25:44.660 align:middle line:90%
on the other.

00:25:44.660 --> 00:25:46.550 align:middle line:84%
We need to think about
both of those things

00:25:46.550 --> 00:25:50.120 align:middle line:84%
if we're going to describe
what the language is doing.

00:25:50.120 --> 00:25:53.180 align:middle line:84%
This is a classic
test from 1957.

00:25:53.180 --> 00:25:54.733 align:middle line:84%
I mentioned it in
class before, but I

00:25:54.733 --> 00:25:56.900 align:middle line:84%
wanted to talk about it a
little more now that we've

00:25:56.900 --> 00:25:58.790 align:middle line:90%
drawn this distinction.

00:25:58.790 --> 00:26:04.070 align:middle line:84%
So Jean Berko, later
Jean Berko Gleason,

00:26:04.070 --> 00:26:07.700 align:middle line:84%
did this work in
1957 with children.

00:26:07.700 --> 00:26:11.360 align:middle line:84%
She was demonstrating that
they know these generalizations

00:26:11.360 --> 00:26:15.420 align:middle line:84%
about plurals even with words
they've never heard before.

00:26:15.420 --> 00:26:17.453 align:middle line:84%
So she would show them
pictures like this one.

00:26:17.453 --> 00:26:18.620 align:middle line:90%
This is one of her pictures.

00:26:18.620 --> 00:26:20.900 align:middle line:90%
She said "This is a wug."

00:26:20.900 --> 00:26:25.430 align:middle line:84%
And then she would show them
two pictures of the same thing.

00:26:25.430 --> 00:26:28.040 align:middle line:84%
And she would say, "Now
there are two of them.

00:26:28.040 --> 00:26:35.840 align:middle line:84%
There are two"-- and children
were supposed to say "wugs"

00:26:35.840 --> 00:26:38.410 align:middle line:84%
even though they'd never heard
anybody say "wug" before,

00:26:38.410 --> 00:26:38.910 align:middle line:90%
right?

00:26:38.910 --> 00:26:42.680 align:middle line:84%
So Jean Berko Gleason, Jean
Berko made up a bunch of words.

00:26:42.680 --> 00:26:45.110 align:middle line:84%
She worked hard to make sure
they were not real words.

00:26:45.110 --> 00:26:47.660 align:middle line:84%
And then she drew
many cute pictures

00:26:47.660 --> 00:26:50.650 align:middle line:84%
and showed children singulars
and plurals and said,

00:26:50.650 --> 00:26:51.650 align:middle line:90%
OK, here's the singular.

00:26:51.650 --> 00:26:52.790 align:middle line:90%
What's the plural?

00:26:52.790 --> 00:26:55.352 align:middle line:90%
And the kids responded.

00:26:55.352 --> 00:26:56.810 align:middle line:84%
And she wrote down
their responses.

00:26:56.810 --> 00:26:58.790 align:middle line:84%
And their responses,
interestingly,

00:26:58.790 --> 00:27:02.930 align:middle line:84%
were accurate in one way,
and sometimes inaccurate

00:27:02.930 --> 00:27:05.187 align:middle line:84%
in another, which is
interesting to think about.

00:27:05.187 --> 00:27:07.520 align:middle line:84%
So she was working with
children who were five years old

00:27:07.520 --> 00:27:09.483 align:middle line:84%
and children who
were seven years old.

00:27:09.483 --> 00:27:11.150 align:middle line:84%
What she discovered
was that by the time

00:27:11.150 --> 00:27:13.108 align:middle line:84%
they were five years old,
they are, in a sense,

00:27:13.108 --> 00:27:17.660 align:middle line:84%
perfect, in the sense that all
of the words that they came up

00:27:17.660 --> 00:27:21.600 align:middle line:84%
with were phonotactically
well formed English words.

00:27:21.600 --> 00:27:24.860 align:middle line:84%
So here are three of
Berko's words-- "hif,"

00:27:24.860 --> 00:27:27.370 align:middle line:90%
"fas," and "muz," yeah?

00:27:27.370 --> 00:27:31.100 align:middle line:84%
And I think everybody
did "wug" perfectly.

00:27:31.100 --> 00:27:33.830 align:middle line:84%
Everybody said the plural
of "wug" is "wugs."

00:27:33.830 --> 00:27:37.310 align:middle line:84%
But there were other cases
where children would say,

00:27:37.310 --> 00:27:40.820 align:middle line:84%
sometimes, things like the
plural of "hif" is "hifes,"

00:27:40.820 --> 00:27:44.540 align:middle line:84%
or the plural of "fas" is
"fas," or the plural of "muz"

00:27:44.540 --> 00:27:47.740 align:middle line:90%
is "muz."

00:27:47.740 --> 00:27:50.178 align:middle line:90%
These are all words where--

00:27:50.178 --> 00:27:51.970 align:middle line:84%
and then there were
other kinds of mistakes

00:27:51.970 --> 00:27:53.110 align:middle line:90%
that children didn't make.

00:27:53.110 --> 00:27:58.000 align:middle line:84%
So nobody said that the
plural "wug" was "wuks,"

00:27:58.000 --> 00:28:02.830 align:middle line:84%
or that the plural of
"wook" is "wookz," right?

00:28:02.830 --> 00:28:07.630 align:middle line:84%
Or that the plural of
fas is "fas-z," yeah?

00:28:07.630 --> 00:28:08.830 align:middle line:90%
What were children doing?

00:28:08.830 --> 00:28:11.170 align:middle line:84%
Well, children know
if you show them

00:28:11.170 --> 00:28:15.010 align:middle line:84%
something that's underlyingly--
you give them "hif," which

00:28:15.010 --> 00:28:17.595 align:middle line:84%
for some reason I'm spelling
backwards-- you give them "hif"

00:28:17.595 --> 00:28:18.970 align:middle line:84%
and you ask them
the plural, they

00:28:18.970 --> 00:28:21.610 align:middle line:84%
know that you're
supposed to add a "z."

00:28:21.610 --> 00:28:24.220 align:middle line:84%
And they know that English
doesn't allow words

00:28:24.220 --> 00:28:28.000 align:middle line:84%
that end in a "z" preceded
by a voiceless sound.

00:28:28.000 --> 00:28:29.920 align:middle line:84%
But some of these five
and seven-year-olds

00:28:29.920 --> 00:28:32.290 align:middle line:84%
were a little fuzzy on
the possible repairs.

00:28:32.290 --> 00:28:35.290 align:middle line:90%


00:28:35.290 --> 00:28:38.740 align:middle line:84%
None of them said, "Oh,
yeah, It's hifz," right?

00:28:38.740 --> 00:28:40.930 align:middle line:90%
But I just said this.

00:28:40.930 --> 00:28:43.420 align:middle line:84%
If the only problem
is, well, this word

00:28:43.420 --> 00:28:44.920 align:middle line:84%
ends in a voiceless
sound followed

00:28:44.920 --> 00:28:47.590 align:middle line:84%
by a "z," in fact,
what you're supposed

00:28:47.590 --> 00:28:49.360 align:middle line:90%
to do is devoice the "z."

00:28:49.360 --> 00:28:50.890 align:middle line:90%
That's what an adult would do.

00:28:50.890 --> 00:28:53.290 align:middle line:84%
So the plural of
"hif" is "hifs," yeah?

00:28:53.290 --> 00:28:57.730 align:middle line:84%
But if you were instead
to insert a schwa,

00:28:57.730 --> 00:28:59.860 align:middle line:84%
well, that would
fix it, too, right?

00:28:59.860 --> 00:29:03.670 align:middle line:84%
So now the "z" is preceded
by a voiced sound.

00:29:03.670 --> 00:29:05.322 align:middle line:84%
The plural of "hif"
is "hif-ez," yeah?

00:29:05.322 --> 00:29:07.780 align:middle line:84%
And there were some kids who
were going through a stage who

00:29:07.780 --> 00:29:10.180 align:middle line:84%
were entertaining that
possibility, that that was

00:29:10.180 --> 00:29:12.160 align:middle line:90%
the correct repair for that.

00:29:12.160 --> 00:29:18.160 align:middle line:84%
So what Berko found was
that children universally

00:29:18.160 --> 00:29:20.200 align:middle line:90%
knew the phonotactics, right?

00:29:20.200 --> 00:29:23.500 align:middle line:84%
They knew that English
doesn't allow words

00:29:23.500 --> 00:29:25.930 align:middle line:84%
that end in a "z" preceded
by a voiceless sound.

00:29:25.930 --> 00:29:31.150 align:middle line:84%
But they made intriguing
mistakes of various kinds--

00:29:31.150 --> 00:29:32.192 align:middle line:90%
not very often.

00:29:32.192 --> 00:29:34.150 align:middle line:84%
Most of the kids just
did everything perfectly.

00:29:34.150 --> 00:29:35.140 align:middle line:84%
But every so often,
she would get

00:29:35.140 --> 00:29:36.340 align:middle line:90%
kids who made some mistakes.

00:29:36.340 --> 00:29:38.560 align:middle line:84%
And the mistakes
were of this class.

00:29:38.560 --> 00:29:41.590 align:middle line:84%
That is, mistakes
where they knew

00:29:41.590 --> 00:29:45.110 align:middle line:84%
that there needed to be a
repair but got the repair wrong.

00:29:45.110 --> 00:29:48.970 align:middle line:84%
Similarly for "muz,"
this was a kid who--

00:29:48.970 --> 00:29:52.570 align:middle line:84%
the singular is "muz,"
and the kid knows

00:29:52.570 --> 00:29:53.830 align:middle line:90%
that you need to add a "z."

00:29:53.830 --> 00:29:55.955 align:middle line:84%
And the kid knows that you
can't have two stridents

00:29:55.955 --> 00:29:56.980 align:middle line:90%
at the end of a word.

00:29:56.980 --> 00:29:59.140 align:middle line:84%
The correct answer,
the adult's answer,

00:29:59.140 --> 00:30:01.810 align:middle line:84%
is to insert a schwa
so the plural of "muz"

00:30:01.810 --> 00:30:02.770 align:middle line:90%
will be "muzes."

00:30:02.770 --> 00:30:08.050 align:middle line:84%
But deleting the "z," one of the
"z"s-- hard to know which one--

00:30:08.050 --> 00:30:09.590 align:middle line:90%
also fixes that problem.

00:30:09.590 --> 00:30:11.800 align:middle line:84%
And so that was a
kid who thought,

00:30:11.800 --> 00:30:13.720 align:middle line:90%
yeah, I'll delete a "z," yeah?

00:30:13.720 --> 00:30:14.938 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, Joseph?

00:30:14.938 --> 00:30:17.900 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Is the deletion
of a consonant viewed

00:30:17.900 --> 00:30:19.260 align:middle line:90%
as [INAUDIBLE]?

00:30:19.260 --> 00:30:21.260 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: As
something that English does?

00:30:21.260 --> 00:30:22.010 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, no.

00:30:22.010 --> 00:30:24.020 align:middle line:84%
So English-- there
aren't, I don't think,

00:30:24.020 --> 00:30:30.050 align:middle line:84%
any English nouns that form
their plural by adding a "z"

00:30:30.050 --> 00:30:31.795 align:middle line:84%
and deleting something
before the "z."

00:30:31.795 --> 00:30:34.880 align:middle line:84%
I don't believe
that ever happens.

00:30:34.880 --> 00:30:36.860 align:middle line:84%
This is me slowing
down while I try

00:30:36.860 --> 00:30:39.410 align:middle line:84%
to think of English nouns,
of which I know several,

00:30:39.410 --> 00:30:42.710 align:middle line:84%
but I can't think
of any that do that.

00:30:42.710 --> 00:30:43.730 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:30:43.730 --> 00:30:44.960 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:30:44.960 --> 00:30:46.490 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:30:46.490 --> 00:30:48.230 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, so is this clear?

00:30:48.230 --> 00:30:49.970 align:middle line:84%
Another reason to
draw this distinction

00:30:49.970 --> 00:30:52.970 align:middle line:84%
between, on the one hand, what
I was calling phonotactics,

00:30:52.970 --> 00:30:56.010 align:middle line:84%
these rules for which kinds
of sounds can be where,

00:30:56.010 --> 00:30:57.900 align:middle line:84%
and on the other
hand, the repairs--

00:30:57.900 --> 00:31:01.130 align:middle line:84%
so the kids that
Berko was working with

00:31:01.130 --> 00:31:04.100 align:middle line:84%
knew the phonotactics, but
were sometimes fuzzy on what

00:31:04.100 --> 00:31:06.683 align:middle line:90%
the correct repairs were.

00:31:06.683 --> 00:31:08.570 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:31:08.570 --> 00:31:10.220 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:31:10.220 --> 00:31:12.853 align:middle line:84%
So, moral-- yet again, need
to distinguish between,

00:31:12.853 --> 00:31:14.270 align:middle line:84%
on the one hand,
the phonotactics,

00:31:14.270 --> 00:31:16.670 align:middle line:84%
and on the other hand, the
particular sound changes,

00:31:16.670 --> 00:31:18.630 align:middle line:84%
the repairs that enforce
the phonotactics.

00:31:18.630 --> 00:31:24.160 align:middle line:84%
It's apparently possible to know
one of those but not the other.

00:31:24.160 --> 00:31:25.200 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:31:25.200 --> 00:31:28.370 align:middle line:90%
That ends the chapter.

00:31:28.370 --> 00:31:31.337 align:middle line:84%
Now we're going to talk
about something else.

00:31:31.337 --> 00:31:32.920 align:middle line:84%
So are there any
questions about that?

00:31:32.920 --> 00:31:34.132 align:middle line:90%
Is that clear?

00:31:34.132 --> 00:31:35.980 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:31:35.980 --> 00:31:38.980 align:middle line:84%
We've already done some of
this, but I want to do more.

00:31:38.980 --> 00:31:42.730 align:middle line:84%
We've been talking
about how there

00:31:42.730 --> 00:31:46.840 align:middle line:84%
are some kinds of sound changes
that are natural because they

00:31:46.840 --> 00:31:48.940 align:middle line:84%
apply to natural
classes of things

00:31:48.940 --> 00:31:53.500 align:middle line:90%
that can be almost circular.

00:31:53.500 --> 00:31:57.910 align:middle line:84%
So I quickly introduced
you to the word stridents

00:31:57.910 --> 00:32:00.700 align:middle line:84%
because the generalization
about English plurals

00:32:00.700 --> 00:32:04.210 align:middle line:84%
is that two stridents can't
end a word in English.

00:32:04.210 --> 00:32:07.060 align:middle line:84%
And that's why you introduce
a schwa just in case

00:32:07.060 --> 00:32:08.830 align:middle line:84%
you're in danger of
having two stridents

00:32:08.830 --> 00:32:12.370 align:middle line:90%
at the end of your word, yeah.

00:32:12.370 --> 00:32:15.850 align:middle line:84%
The word strident has
an acoustic definition.

00:32:15.850 --> 00:32:19.420 align:middle line:84%
They are fricatives that have
lots of high frequency noise.

00:32:19.420 --> 00:32:22.990 align:middle line:84%
But it's of interest
that there are phenomena

00:32:22.990 --> 00:32:25.480 align:middle line:84%
like the allomorphy
in English plurals

00:32:25.480 --> 00:32:28.030 align:middle line:84%
that make reference
to that acoustic fact.

00:32:28.030 --> 00:32:31.720 align:middle line:84%
That's apparently something
that a language can care about--

00:32:31.720 --> 00:32:33.983 align:middle line:84%
how much high frequency
noise you have at once.

00:32:33.983 --> 00:32:36.400 align:middle line:84%
It's also something that varies
from language to language.

00:32:36.400 --> 00:32:38.830 align:middle line:84%
There are languages that
are perfectly happy to have

00:32:38.830 --> 00:32:40.690 align:middle line:90%
long strings of stridents.

00:32:40.690 --> 00:32:42.940 align:middle line:90%
English just isn't one of them.

00:32:42.940 --> 00:32:45.430 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:32:45.430 --> 00:32:49.050 align:middle line:84%
So we've already done, as I
said, some talking about this.

00:32:49.050 --> 00:32:51.160 align:middle line:84%
So when I was first
introducing you to consonants

00:32:51.160 --> 00:32:53.470 align:middle line:84%
or introducing you
to vowels, you'd

00:32:53.470 --> 00:32:55.570 align:middle line:84%
already met consonants at
some point in your life.

00:32:55.570 --> 00:32:59.170 align:middle line:84%
But we were talking about them
in a particular systematic way.

00:32:59.170 --> 00:33:04.060 align:middle line:84%
We talked about various ways
of classifying them, saying

00:33:04.060 --> 00:33:06.340 align:middle line:84%
there are some consonants
that are fricatives

00:33:06.340 --> 00:33:08.020 align:middle line:84%
and some consonants
that are stops,

00:33:08.020 --> 00:33:10.210 align:middle line:84%
or saying there are some
consonants that are voiced

00:33:10.210 --> 00:33:12.410 align:middle line:84%
and some consonants
that are voiceless.

00:33:12.410 --> 00:33:14.920 align:middle line:84%
So what I want to do
today is to introduce you

00:33:14.920 --> 00:33:16.930 align:middle line:84%
to some other kinds
of classification.

00:33:16.930 --> 00:33:18.663 align:middle line:84%
And I'll, as much
as possible, try

00:33:18.663 --> 00:33:20.080 align:middle line:84%
to show you places
where languages

00:33:20.080 --> 00:33:23.500 align:middle line:84%
care about a particular
class of sounds.

00:33:23.500 --> 00:33:27.340 align:middle line:84%
Again, this is all in the
service of making it easier

00:33:27.340 --> 00:33:30.190 align:middle line:84%
for us to understand why
certain kinds of sound changes

00:33:30.190 --> 00:33:35.320 align:middle line:90%
are natural and others are not.

00:33:35.320 --> 00:33:35.820 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:33:35.820 --> 00:33:39.840 align:middle line:84%
So here's one that
we've already seen.

00:33:39.840 --> 00:33:41.175 align:middle line:90%
There are sounds that are nasal.

00:33:41.175 --> 00:33:43.880 align:middle line:90%


00:33:43.880 --> 00:33:46.085 align:middle line:84%
This is a contrast
that I believe

00:33:46.085 --> 00:33:48.710 align:middle line:84%
I have introduced you to before,
but let me introduce you to it

00:33:48.710 --> 00:33:49.280 align:middle line:90%
again.

00:33:49.280 --> 00:33:51.650 align:middle line:90%
There's a distinction--

00:33:51.650 --> 00:33:53.750 align:middle line:84%
I think I brought this
up last time, actually.

00:33:53.750 --> 00:33:56.780 align:middle line:84%
There's a distinction, when
we talk about consonants,

00:33:56.780 --> 00:33:59.660 align:middle line:84%
between what are
called sonorants

00:33:59.660 --> 00:34:01.650 align:middle line:90%
and what are called obstruents.

00:34:01.650 --> 00:34:03.530 align:middle line:84%
So these are opposites
of each other.

00:34:03.530 --> 00:34:06.540 align:middle line:84%
I hope I have the word
"obstruent" on a later slide.

00:34:06.540 --> 00:34:10.580 align:middle line:84%
But just in case I don't, there
are these two kinds of sounds--

00:34:10.580 --> 00:34:14.840 align:middle line:90%
obstruents and sonorants.

00:34:14.840 --> 00:34:16.820 align:middle line:84%
And the distinction
between them has

00:34:16.820 --> 00:34:19.699 align:middle line:84%
to do with whether
you are building up

00:34:19.699 --> 00:34:22.110 align:middle line:90%
pressure in the oral cavity.

00:34:22.110 --> 00:34:26.989 align:middle line:84%
So the sonorants are the
nasals and the liquids

00:34:26.989 --> 00:34:29.929 align:middle line:84%
and the glides, the
consonants in which there's

00:34:29.929 --> 00:34:33.139 align:middle line:84%
no pressure building
up in the oral cavity,

00:34:33.139 --> 00:34:35.150 align:middle line:84%
whereas the
obstruents-- those are

00:34:35.150 --> 00:34:38.690 align:middle line:84%
the stops and the fricatives,
the oral stops and fricatives.

00:34:38.690 --> 00:34:41.270 align:middle line:84%
Those are the sounds in which
there is pressure building up

00:34:41.270 --> 00:34:42.260 align:middle line:90%
in the oral cavity.

00:34:42.260 --> 00:34:45.260 align:middle line:84%
For a stop, it's because, well,
you've stopped the flow of air

00:34:45.260 --> 00:34:46.159 align:middle line:90%
at the mouth.

00:34:46.159 --> 00:34:47.719 align:middle line:90%
And so pressure is building up.

00:34:47.719 --> 00:34:49.302 align:middle line:84%
And for a fricative,
well, you haven't

00:34:49.302 --> 00:34:51.469 align:middle line:84%
stopped the flow of air,
but you're constricting it.

00:34:51.469 --> 00:34:53.000 align:middle line:90%
That's how you make a fricative.

00:34:53.000 --> 00:34:55.469 align:middle line:84%
And so pressure is building
up in the oral cavity.

00:34:55.469 --> 00:34:58.190 align:middle line:84%
And when I say pressure is
building up in the oral cavity,

00:34:58.190 --> 00:35:02.180 align:middle line:84%
what I mean is that phoneticists
have put instruments

00:35:02.180 --> 00:35:05.600 align:middle line:84%
inside people's mouths to
measure air pressure to find

00:35:05.600 --> 00:35:06.440 align:middle line:90%
out what happens.

00:35:06.440 --> 00:35:09.730 align:middle line:84%
And they have discovered
that this is true, yeah.

00:35:09.730 --> 00:35:11.963 align:middle line:84%
So I try not to lie
to you in this class

00:35:11.963 --> 00:35:13.130 align:middle line:90%
any more than I can help it.

00:35:13.130 --> 00:35:14.990 align:middle line:84%
But in this particular
case, what I'm saying

00:35:14.990 --> 00:35:15.890 align:middle line:90%
is absolutely true.

00:35:15.890 --> 00:35:19.285 align:middle line:90%


00:35:19.285 --> 00:35:22.100 align:middle line:84%
OK, so there are sonorants
and there are obstruents.

00:35:22.100 --> 00:35:24.500 align:middle line:84%
We have already talked about
the classification of sounds

00:35:24.500 --> 00:35:25.910 align:middle line:84%
into voiced and
voiceless sounds.

00:35:25.910 --> 00:35:28.760 align:middle line:84%
So the red sounds on
this slide are voiced.

00:35:28.760 --> 00:35:30.620 align:middle line:84%
And as you can see, in
this cross-cut-- some

00:35:30.620 --> 00:35:31.828 align:middle line:90%
of the other classifications.

00:35:31.828 --> 00:35:37.430 align:middle line:84%
So you can be an obstruent,
like a stop or a fricative,

00:35:37.430 --> 00:35:40.140 align:middle line:84%
and you can be either
voiced or voiceless.

00:35:40.140 --> 00:35:43.460 align:middle line:84%
So English stops can
be voiced or voiceless.

00:35:43.460 --> 00:35:46.220 align:middle line:84%
English fricatives can
be voiced or voiceless.

00:35:46.220 --> 00:35:48.110 align:middle line:84%
English sonorants,
interestingly,

00:35:48.110 --> 00:35:49.430 align:middle line:90%
can only be voiced.

00:35:49.430 --> 00:35:51.410 align:middle line:90%
They cannot be voiceless.

00:35:51.410 --> 00:35:54.830 align:middle line:84%
We have sonorants
like "l," like "l,"

00:35:54.830 --> 00:35:56.865 align:middle line:84%
but we don't have a
voiceless version of "l."

00:35:56.865 --> 00:35:58.490 align:middle line:84%
We talked about this
when we were first

00:35:58.490 --> 00:36:00.253 align:middle line:90%
going through sounds.

00:36:00.253 --> 00:36:01.670 align:middle line:84%
There are languages
out there that

00:36:01.670 --> 00:36:05.570 align:middle line:84%
have a voiceless version of
"l," that have a "lh" sound.

00:36:05.570 --> 00:36:07.320 align:middle line:90%
Tibetan has one, for example.

00:36:07.320 --> 00:36:10.280 align:middle line:84%
So if you go to Lhasa,
the capital of Tibet,

00:36:10.280 --> 00:36:13.490 align:middle line:84%
you must learn to
make voiceless "l"s.

00:36:13.490 --> 00:36:16.760 align:middle line:90%
Welsh has a voiceless "l.".

00:36:16.760 --> 00:36:18.560 align:middle line:90%
They spell it with two "l"s.

00:36:18.560 --> 00:36:21.770 align:middle line:84%
So if you look at Welsh words,
you'll see pairs of "l"s.

00:36:21.770 --> 00:36:25.250 align:middle line:84%
That's their way of
spelling a voiceless "l."

00:36:25.250 --> 00:36:29.810 align:middle line:84%
So there's a traditional
Welsh name spelled like this

00:36:29.810 --> 00:36:32.660 align:middle line:84%
and pronounced
"Lhoyd," which has

00:36:32.660 --> 00:36:37.070 align:middle line:84%
been borrowed into English twice
as the name Lloyd and the name

00:36:37.070 --> 00:36:39.110 align:middle line:90%
Floyd.

00:36:39.110 --> 00:36:40.637 align:middle line:84%
These are both
attempts to pronounce

00:36:40.637 --> 00:36:42.470 align:middle line:84%
"Lhoyd," which we can't
pronounce because we

00:36:42.470 --> 00:36:45.270 align:middle line:90%
don't have that sound.

00:36:45.270 --> 00:36:45.770 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:36:45.770 --> 00:36:50.065 align:middle line:84%
So English has voiced and
voiceless obstruents, stops

00:36:50.065 --> 00:36:50.690 align:middle line:90%
and fricatives.

00:36:50.690 --> 00:36:52.490 align:middle line:90%
But it only has voice sonorants.

00:36:52.490 --> 00:36:55.160 align:middle line:84%
That's a very common
state of affairs.

00:36:55.160 --> 00:36:56.930 align:middle line:84%
If you're going
to have a voicing

00:36:56.930 --> 00:37:00.770 align:middle line:84%
distinction in only one of these
places, it's in the obstruents.

00:37:00.770 --> 00:37:02.660 align:middle line:84%
Languages only having
voiced sonorants--

00:37:02.660 --> 00:37:06.860 align:middle line:84%
that's very common, though
not universal, as we've said.

00:37:06.860 --> 00:37:09.540 align:middle line:90%


00:37:09.540 --> 00:37:13.670 align:middle line:84%
Quick interlude to use
one of these features.

00:37:13.670 --> 00:37:18.510 align:middle line:84%
Here are a bunch of verbs that
you can make out of adjectives.

00:37:18.510 --> 00:37:21.410 align:middle line:84%
So you can take an
adjective like "black."

00:37:21.410 --> 00:37:23.867 align:middle line:84%
And you can make
a verb, "blacken,"

00:37:23.867 --> 00:37:26.450 align:middle line:84%
which can be either a transitive
verb or in intransitive verb,

00:37:26.450 --> 00:37:26.950 align:middle line:90%
I guess.

00:37:26.950 --> 00:37:33.650 align:middle line:84%
So you can blacken the paper
or blacken the fish, I guess.

00:37:33.650 --> 00:37:37.190 align:middle line:90%
It means to make it black.

00:37:37.190 --> 00:37:43.190 align:middle line:84%
Similarly, something can whiten
or lessen or freshen or darken.

00:37:43.190 --> 00:37:45.230 align:middle line:84%
You can offer to
freshen a drink, say.

00:37:45.230 --> 00:37:48.020 align:middle line:84%
It means to make
it fresh, right?

00:37:48.020 --> 00:37:51.290 align:middle line:84%
So there's this "-en" that you
can add to adjectives to make

00:37:51.290 --> 00:37:51.920 align:middle line:90%
verbs.

00:37:51.920 --> 00:37:54.870 align:middle line:90%
Does that sound right?

00:37:54.870 --> 00:37:59.570 align:middle line:84%
On the other hand, there are
these other adjectives which

00:37:59.570 --> 00:38:01.250 align:middle line:90%
can't have "-en," yeah?

00:38:01.250 --> 00:38:07.790 align:middle line:84%
So the screen of a
computer can darken,

00:38:07.790 --> 00:38:11.310 align:middle line:90%
but it cannot "yellowen," yeah?

00:38:11.310 --> 00:38:14.390 align:middle line:90%
A piece of paper that gets old--

00:38:14.390 --> 00:38:16.160 align:middle line:84%
what you say is
that the yellows.

00:38:16.160 --> 00:38:17.540 align:middle line:90%
It yellowed with age.

00:38:17.540 --> 00:38:20.000 align:middle line:84%
You don't say that it
"yellowened," yeah?

00:38:20.000 --> 00:38:22.670 align:middle line:84%
Similarly, you can't
"dimmen" or "grayen"

00:38:22.670 --> 00:38:24.593 align:middle line:90%
or "clearen" or "brownen."

00:38:24.593 --> 00:38:26.510 align:middle line:84%
All of those things--
if you want to make them

00:38:26.510 --> 00:38:27.860 align:middle line:90%
into verbs, you just [SNAPS].

00:38:27.860 --> 00:38:30.010 align:middle line:90%
They're verbs, yeah?

00:38:30.010 --> 00:38:30.670 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:38:30.670 --> 00:38:32.770 align:middle line:90%
Does that sound right?

00:38:32.770 --> 00:38:34.840 align:middle line:84%
What's the difference
between "black,"

00:38:34.840 --> 00:38:38.980 align:middle line:84%
"white," "less," "fresh,"
and "dark" on the one hand,

00:38:38.980 --> 00:38:43.090 align:middle line:84%
and "yellow," "dim,"
"gray," "clear,"

00:38:43.090 --> 00:38:46.843 align:middle line:90%
and "brown" on the other?

00:38:46.843 --> 00:38:52.402 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: They end with voiceless
stops in the first line?

00:38:52.402 --> 00:38:53.860 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
In the first line,

00:38:53.860 --> 00:38:57.490 align:middle line:84%
"black," "white," and "dark"
end with voiceless stops.

00:38:57.490 --> 00:39:02.190 align:middle line:84%
And "less" and "fresh" end with
voiceless fricatives, yeah?

00:39:02.190 --> 00:39:03.000 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, yeah.

00:39:03.000 --> 00:39:06.340 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, nice.

00:39:06.340 --> 00:39:11.375 align:middle line:84%
And "yellow," "dim," "gray,"
"clear," and "brown" end in

00:39:11.375 --> 00:39:12.085 align:middle line:90%
voiced sounds.

00:39:12.085 --> 00:39:14.600 align:middle line:90%


00:39:14.600 --> 00:39:18.977 align:middle line:84%
What's the cover term
for stops and fricatives?

00:39:18.977 --> 00:39:19.810 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: Obstruents.

00:39:19.810 --> 00:39:21.490 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Obstruents.

00:39:21.490 --> 00:39:26.260 align:middle line:84%
So "-en" can be added to
words ending in obstruents.

00:39:26.260 --> 00:39:28.990 align:middle line:84%
And it can't be
added to adjectives

00:39:28.990 --> 00:39:30.730 align:middle line:90%
ending in sonorants, yeah?

00:39:30.730 --> 00:39:34.290 align:middle line:90%


00:39:34.290 --> 00:39:38.280 align:middle line:84%
Another place where it's
useful to distinguish sonorants

00:39:38.280 --> 00:39:40.950 align:middle line:90%
from obstruents--

00:39:40.950 --> 00:39:43.740 align:middle line:84%
Polish plurals-- I think this
is the last time I will say

00:39:43.740 --> 00:39:45.360 align:middle line:90%
anything about Polish plurals.

00:39:45.360 --> 00:39:48.910 align:middle line:84%
I can't promise because
it's a very useful example.

00:39:48.910 --> 00:39:51.660 align:middle line:84%
But if you're getting tired of
Polish plurals, I apologize.

00:39:51.660 --> 00:39:53.160 align:middle line:84%
I think we're just
about to the end.

00:39:53.160 --> 00:39:55.950 align:middle line:84%
On the other hand, if you've
become fond of Polish plurals,

00:39:55.950 --> 00:39:59.190 align:middle line:84%
please gaze at this
slide for a while.

00:39:59.190 --> 00:40:02.050 align:middle line:90%
This is your last big chance.

00:40:02.050 --> 00:40:04.470 align:middle line:84%
What we said about
Polish plurals was

00:40:04.470 --> 00:40:06.480 align:middle line:84%
that it's useful
to say that there

00:40:06.480 --> 00:40:09.870 align:middle line:84%
are words that end underlyingly
in sounds like "g" and "b"

00:40:09.870 --> 00:40:10.620 align:middle line:90%
and "z."

00:40:10.620 --> 00:40:12.960 align:middle line:84%
And those final
sounds, "g" and "b"

00:40:12.960 --> 00:40:15.300 align:middle line:84%
and "z," devoice when
they're at the ends of words.

00:40:15.300 --> 00:40:18.210 align:middle line:84%
So underlying "wuk,"
the word for "lye,"

00:40:18.210 --> 00:40:21.670 align:middle line:84%
becomes "wuk" if you don't
add anything after it.

00:40:21.670 --> 00:40:23.070 align:middle line:84%
And same with the
word for "club"

00:40:23.070 --> 00:40:24.630 align:middle line:90%
and the word for "rubble."

00:40:24.630 --> 00:40:26.580 align:middle line:84%
And I have been
incautiously saying things

00:40:26.580 --> 00:40:33.130 align:middle line:84%
like, "Polish devoices its final
consonants," which was a lie.

00:40:33.130 --> 00:40:36.000 align:middle line:84%
I think I told you just
now that I try not to lie

00:40:36.000 --> 00:40:38.970 align:middle line:90%
to you any more than I have to.

00:40:38.970 --> 00:40:41.730 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't devoice its
final consonants, right?

00:40:41.730 --> 00:40:44.910 align:middle line:84%
Because Polish has
words like "dom,"

00:40:44.910 --> 00:40:46.440 align:middle line:90%
which is the word for house.

00:40:46.440 --> 00:40:48.576 align:middle line:84%
And that ends in a
voiced sound, right?

00:40:48.576 --> 00:40:50.826 align:middle line:84%
"Dom" doesn't become "dom"
[pronounced with voicelesss

00:40:50.826 --> 00:40:52.860 align:middle line:90%
"m"], yeah?

00:40:52.860 --> 00:40:54.880 align:middle line:90%
Right?

00:40:54.880 --> 00:40:57.010 align:middle line:84%
So it's not the case
that final consonants

00:40:57.010 --> 00:40:59.568 align:middle line:90%
become voiceless in Polish.

00:40:59.568 --> 00:41:01.735 align:middle line:84%
What are the things that
become voiceless in Polish?

00:41:01.735 --> 00:41:04.690 align:middle line:90%


00:41:04.690 --> 00:41:05.620 align:middle line:90%
Obstruents, yeah?

00:41:05.620 --> 00:41:07.150 align:middle line:90%
Stops and fricatives.

00:41:07.150 --> 00:41:09.790 align:middle line:84%
Those are the things that
become voiceless in Polish.

00:41:09.790 --> 00:41:13.540 align:middle line:84%
Relatedly, Polish is like
English in having voiced

00:41:13.540 --> 00:41:17.170 align:middle line:84%
and voiceless obstruents,
but not having

00:41:17.170 --> 00:41:18.850 align:middle line:90%
voiced and voiceless sonorants.

00:41:18.850 --> 00:41:23.170 align:middle line:84%
So Polish-- the "m," in a sense,
doesn't have anywhere to go.

00:41:23.170 --> 00:41:24.580 align:middle line:90%
Polish doesn't have that sound.

00:41:24.580 --> 00:41:27.970 align:middle line:90%


00:41:27.970 --> 00:41:29.270 align:middle line:90%
Common situation.

00:41:29.270 --> 00:41:32.590 align:middle line:84%
So it's really final obstruents
that are becoming voiceless.

00:41:32.590 --> 00:41:36.110 align:middle line:90%


00:41:36.110 --> 00:41:37.460 align:middle line:90%
OK, all right.

00:41:37.460 --> 00:41:41.030 align:middle line:84%
So that's a reason
to want to care

00:41:41.030 --> 00:41:44.235 align:middle line:84%
about the distinction between
sonorants and obstruents,

00:41:44.235 --> 00:41:45.860 align:middle line:84%
not just because you
want to understand

00:41:45.860 --> 00:41:48.830 align:middle line:84%
why it's "whiten" and
"darken" and "lessen,"

00:41:48.830 --> 00:41:51.525 align:middle line:90%
but not "yellowen."

00:41:51.525 --> 00:41:53.150 align:middle line:84%
It seems to be a
distinction that cares

00:41:53.150 --> 00:41:54.630 align:middle line:90%
about sonorants and obstruents.

00:41:54.630 --> 00:41:56.090 align:middle line:90%
Here's another one.

00:41:56.090 --> 00:41:58.760 align:middle line:84%
Polish, which as I've
said now several times,

00:41:58.760 --> 00:42:00.800 align:middle line:84%
and I think this might
be the last time--

00:42:00.800 --> 00:42:03.558 align:middle line:84%
Polish is doing something that
is very common in languages

00:42:03.558 --> 00:42:04.100 align:middle line:90%
of the world.

00:42:04.100 --> 00:42:07.850 align:middle line:90%
It's devoicing final obstruents.

00:42:07.850 --> 00:42:09.650 align:middle line:84%
That's a thing
that happens a lot.

00:42:09.650 --> 00:42:12.980 align:middle line:90%


00:42:12.980 --> 00:42:13.480 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:42:13.480 --> 00:42:17.740 align:middle line:84%
So phonology so far--
we've been doing phonology

00:42:17.740 --> 00:42:20.050 align:middle line:90%
for a couple of days now.

00:42:20.050 --> 00:42:25.330 align:middle line:84%
It's useful to think of sounds
as undergoing changes sometimes

00:42:25.330 --> 00:42:29.030 align:middle line:84%
driven by sounds coming in
contact with each other.

00:42:29.030 --> 00:42:32.320 align:middle line:84%
And we can describe these
changes in terms of rules.

00:42:32.320 --> 00:42:35.740 align:middle line:84%
There's the formalism for rules
that I've been showing you.

00:42:35.740 --> 00:42:39.880 align:middle line:84%
So we'll say sound A
changes into sound B.

00:42:39.880 --> 00:42:43.040 align:middle line:84%
The slash introduces
you to the environment.

00:42:43.040 --> 00:42:45.598 align:middle line:84%
This is a description of
what's happening in Polish,

00:42:45.598 --> 00:42:47.140 align:middle line:84%
and Turkish, and
German, and Russian,

00:42:47.140 --> 00:42:48.640 align:middle line:90%
and lots of other languages.

00:42:48.640 --> 00:42:51.760 align:middle line:84%
Final obstruents-- so if
you are an obstruent that

00:42:51.760 --> 00:42:53.560 align:middle line:84%
is something which
is not a sonorant,

00:42:53.560 --> 00:42:56.410 align:middle line:84%
you become something
which is not voiced.

00:42:56.410 --> 00:42:58.840 align:middle line:84%
If you are at the
end of a word--

00:42:58.840 --> 00:43:01.300 align:middle line:84%
that environment
description is the way

00:43:01.300 --> 00:43:02.650 align:middle line:90%
to write at the end of a word.

00:43:02.650 --> 00:43:05.950 align:middle line:84%
That hash symbol is the symbol
for a boundary between words.

00:43:05.950 --> 00:43:09.200 align:middle line:84%
And the underscore is where
the change takes place.

00:43:09.200 --> 00:43:13.270 align:middle line:84%
So this rule says
this change happens

00:43:13.270 --> 00:43:16.660 align:middle line:84%
when the sound is
before a word boundary,

00:43:16.660 --> 00:43:19.750 align:middle line:84%
or to put it another way, when
it is at the end of a word.

00:43:19.750 --> 00:43:21.270 align:middle line:90%
Does that make sense?

00:43:21.270 --> 00:43:22.735 align:middle line:90%
This is a way of writing rules.

00:43:22.735 --> 00:43:26.530 align:middle line:90%


00:43:26.530 --> 00:43:29.050 align:middle line:84%
These rules are part of our
knowledge of a language.

00:43:29.050 --> 00:43:30.480 align:middle line:90%
So we've talked about this.

00:43:30.480 --> 00:43:34.260 align:middle line:90%


00:43:34.260 --> 00:43:39.180 align:middle line:84%
As adults, anybody who is
a native speaker of English

00:43:39.180 --> 00:43:42.240 align:middle line:84%
knows how to make
the plural of "wug,"

00:43:42.240 --> 00:43:45.060 align:middle line:84%
or how to make the
plural of "Bach," even.

00:43:45.060 --> 00:43:48.540 align:middle line:84%
So how to make plurals
of words you've

00:43:48.540 --> 00:43:51.540 align:middle line:84%
never heard before and even
words that end in sounds

00:43:51.540 --> 00:43:54.430 align:middle line:84%
that your language
doesn't have, right?

00:43:54.430 --> 00:43:58.350 align:middle line:84%
So if I insist on calling
the composer "Bach"--

00:43:58.350 --> 00:44:01.650 align:middle line:84%
if I insist on the original
Inupiaq pronunciation

00:44:01.650 --> 00:44:05.370 align:middle line:84%
of the word "qaiaq," which
is kayak, a single person

00:44:05.370 --> 00:44:10.810 align:middle line:84%
boat, which in Inupiaq begins
and ends with a uvular stop--

00:44:10.810 --> 00:44:12.810 align:middle line:84%
so if I'm trying to
impress you with my ability

00:44:12.810 --> 00:44:14.550 align:middle line:84%
to make uvular
stops and I insist

00:44:14.550 --> 00:44:17.820 align:middle line:84%
on calling a kayak a "qaiaq,"
then my plural for it

00:44:17.820 --> 00:44:20.370 align:middle line:90%
is going to be "qaiaqs," right?

00:44:20.370 --> 00:44:21.750 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to
add an "s" sound.

00:44:21.750 --> 00:44:24.030 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to devoice it
because that uvular stop,

00:44:24.030 --> 00:44:25.488 align:middle line:84%
although it's a
sound we don't have

00:44:25.488 --> 00:44:27.280 align:middle line:90%
an English-- it's voiceless.

00:44:27.280 --> 00:44:31.140 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to
devoice after it.

00:44:31.140 --> 00:44:33.690 align:middle line:84%
So these rules are part of
our knowledge of a language.

00:44:33.690 --> 00:44:36.540 align:middle line:84%
And we can apply them
to unfamiliar problems,

00:44:36.540 --> 00:44:40.230 align:middle line:84%
unfamiliar words, and
even unfamiliar sounds.

00:44:40.230 --> 00:44:43.455 align:middle line:84%
And I've now tried to
convince you the best

00:44:43.455 --> 00:44:45.330 align:middle line:84%
way to state these rules,
sometimes at least,

00:44:45.330 --> 00:44:46.650 align:middle line:90%
is in terms of features.

00:44:46.650 --> 00:44:49.980 align:middle line:84%
That is, we don't just want
to list the sounds that

00:44:49.980 --> 00:44:51.300 align:middle line:90%
undergo a change.

00:44:51.300 --> 00:44:54.960 align:middle line:84%
We want to have these
features which are ways

00:44:54.960 --> 00:44:57.150 align:middle line:90%
of capturing sets of sounds.

00:44:57.150 --> 00:45:00.840 align:middle line:84%
We think that there are
natural classes of sounds that

00:45:00.840 --> 00:45:02.470 align:middle line:90%
undergo particular changes.

00:45:02.470 --> 00:45:04.560 align:middle line:84%
So there are sound
changes that apply, say,

00:45:04.560 --> 00:45:08.910 align:middle line:84%
to obstruents, to sonorants,
or to vowels, to voiced sounds,

00:45:08.910 --> 00:45:09.957 align:middle line:90%
or to voiceless sounds.

00:45:09.957 --> 00:45:11.790 align:middle line:84%
We're not just going
to list the sounds that

00:45:11.790 --> 00:45:12.707 align:middle line:90%
undergo sound changes.

00:45:12.707 --> 00:45:16.219 align:middle line:84%
We're going to state these
in terms of features, OK?

00:45:16.219 --> 00:45:20.710 align:middle line:90%


00:45:20.710 --> 00:45:23.290 align:middle line:84%
And then the other thing
that I've introduced you to

00:45:23.290 --> 00:45:25.840 align:middle line:84%
is the idea that at
least sometimes, it's

00:45:25.840 --> 00:45:29.740 align:middle line:84%
fruitful to model these
kinds of sound changes

00:45:29.740 --> 00:45:32.500 align:middle line:90%
as involving ranked constraints.

00:45:32.500 --> 00:45:35.770 align:middle line:84%
This was the optimality
theory tableaux--

00:45:35.770 --> 00:45:39.370 align:middle line:84%
that is, to imagine that
languages have a list of things

00:45:39.370 --> 00:45:41.000 align:middle line:90%
that they are trying to achieve.

00:45:41.000 --> 00:45:44.770 align:middle line:84%
And it's a list that's
ranked in order of priority.

00:45:44.770 --> 00:45:47.650 align:middle line:84%
And you get to
observe the interplay

00:45:47.650 --> 00:45:49.420 align:middle line:84%
between these
different priorities

00:45:49.420 --> 00:45:51.610 align:middle line:84%
in certain kinds of
complicated cases.

00:45:51.610 --> 00:45:55.550 align:middle line:84%
The Japanese accent case was one
of the ones that we looked at.

00:45:55.550 --> 00:45:58.280 align:middle line:90%
OK?

00:45:58.280 --> 00:46:00.478 align:middle line:84%
And I think that's it
for this review slide.

00:46:00.478 --> 00:46:01.520 align:middle line:90%
Was there something else?

00:46:01.520 --> 00:46:02.810 align:middle line:90%
Oh, OK.

00:46:02.810 --> 00:46:04.970 align:middle line:84%
Is everyone happy
with all of this?

00:46:04.970 --> 00:46:06.650 align:middle line:84%
Is there any of this that
people are looking at and going,

00:46:06.650 --> 00:46:07.070 align:middle line:90%
no, wait.

00:46:07.070 --> 00:46:08.320 align:middle line:90%
How did-- how did we see that?

00:46:08.320 --> 00:46:10.700 align:middle line:84%
Or what does that mean,
or anything like that?

00:46:10.700 --> 00:46:12.710 align:middle line:84%
Just an attempt to make
sure that we're all

00:46:12.710 --> 00:46:14.240 align:middle line:90%
n the same ballpark here.

00:46:14.240 --> 00:46:15.042 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:46:15.042 --> 00:46:16.280 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Could you clarify
what "feature" means?

00:46:16.280 --> 00:46:17.113 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Oh.

00:46:17.113 --> 00:46:20.660 align:middle line:84%
All I mean by "feature"
are things like voiced,

00:46:20.660 --> 00:46:23.950 align:middle line:84%
or sonorant, or those
kinds of things.

00:46:23.950 --> 00:46:25.370 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, good question.

00:46:25.370 --> 00:46:27.170 align:middle line:84%
And a standard
way to write rules

00:46:27.170 --> 00:46:29.720 align:middle line:84%
involves putting features
in brackets like that.

00:46:29.720 --> 00:46:31.430 align:middle line:84%
So the fancy way
to say something

00:46:31.430 --> 00:46:35.450 align:middle line:84%
is voiceless is to say that
its value for the feature voice

00:46:35.450 --> 00:46:36.530 align:middle line:90%
is minus.

00:46:36.530 --> 00:46:38.480 align:middle line:90%
So it is not voiced, yeah.

00:46:38.480 --> 00:46:41.090 align:middle line:90%
That's all that means.

00:46:41.090 --> 00:46:42.570 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:46:42.570 --> 00:46:43.350 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:46:43.350 --> 00:46:46.200 align:middle line:84%
When we ask you to
do phonology, we

00:46:46.200 --> 00:46:48.840 align:middle line:84%
will tell you
whether we want you--

00:46:48.840 --> 00:46:51.150 align:middle line:84%
to what extent we want
you to be formal about how

00:46:51.150 --> 00:46:52.720 align:middle line:90%
you write your rules.

00:46:52.720 --> 00:46:56.220 align:middle line:84%
So I think the problem
set that I just sent out

00:46:56.220 --> 00:47:00.270 align:middle line:84%
asks you to look around
for possible rules in--

00:47:00.270 --> 00:47:02.020 align:middle line:90%
what is it?

00:47:02.020 --> 00:47:05.820 align:middle line:90%
It's Greenlandic data, I think.

00:47:05.820 --> 00:47:08.970 align:middle line:84%
You don't have to sweat
this level of formality

00:47:08.970 --> 00:47:09.730 align:middle line:90%
in your rules.

00:47:09.730 --> 00:47:11.350 align:middle line:84%
So if your rule for
that problem set--

00:47:11.350 --> 00:47:15.280 align:middle line:84%
so if you think you've
figured out what's going on,

00:47:15.280 --> 00:47:17.720 align:middle line:84%
you can just tell
us, more or less.

00:47:17.720 --> 00:47:19.800 align:middle line:84%
Don't feel like we're
going to count off

00:47:19.800 --> 00:47:24.170 align:middle line:84%
for not using features or not
writing your rules this way.

00:47:24.170 --> 00:47:24.670 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:47:24.670 --> 00:47:28.810 align:middle line:90%


00:47:28.810 --> 00:47:30.610 align:middle line:90%
All right, then.

00:47:30.610 --> 00:47:33.465 align:middle line:90%
Now I told you just now--

00:47:33.465 --> 00:47:34.840 align:middle line:84%
I tried to convince
you earlier--

00:47:34.840 --> 00:47:38.170 align:middle line:90%
that there are cases where--

00:47:38.170 --> 00:47:39.760 align:middle line:84%
the Japanese accent
case was the one

00:47:39.760 --> 00:47:42.100 align:middle line:84%
that we spent the
most time on, where

00:47:42.100 --> 00:47:45.550 align:middle line:84%
it's fruitful to think
of the phenomenon

00:47:45.550 --> 00:47:47.860 align:middle line:84%
as involving a bunch
of ranked priorities,

00:47:47.860 --> 00:47:50.610 align:middle line:84%
a bunch of constraints
that are all pulling

00:47:50.610 --> 00:47:51.610 align:middle line:90%
in different directions.

00:47:51.610 --> 00:47:53.650 align:middle line:84%
And you solve that
problem by deciding

00:47:53.650 --> 00:47:56.260 align:middle line:84%
which is the most
important one, which

00:47:56.260 --> 00:47:58.510 align:middle line:84%
ones are more important than
which other ones, ranking

00:47:58.510 --> 00:48:01.970 align:middle line:84%
your various constraints
with respect to each other.

00:48:01.970 --> 00:48:04.270 align:middle line:84%
There are other
cases, though, that

00:48:04.270 --> 00:48:08.260 align:middle line:84%
are problematic for that way
of thinking about things.

00:48:08.260 --> 00:48:11.560 align:middle line:84%
And there's big literature
on trying to reconcile

00:48:11.560 --> 00:48:12.820 align:middle line:90%
these kinds of observations.

00:48:12.820 --> 00:48:15.400 align:middle line:84%
On the one hand, the
observation that there do

00:48:15.400 --> 00:48:18.490 align:middle line:84%
seem to be these conspiracies
where a language is doing

00:48:18.490 --> 00:48:20.240 align:middle line:84%
the same thing in a
variety of ways, which

00:48:20.240 --> 00:48:22.782 align:middle line:84%
is the kind of thing that makes
people want to think in terms

00:48:22.782 --> 00:48:24.980 align:middle line:84%
of ranked constraints,
and on the other hand,

00:48:24.980 --> 00:48:27.250 align:middle line:84%
the kinds of cases, which
we've talked about before,

00:48:27.250 --> 00:48:29.020 align:middle line:84%
where it looks more
like what's happening

00:48:29.020 --> 00:48:30.580 align:middle line:84%
is that you have
a bunch of rules.

00:48:30.580 --> 00:48:33.040 align:middle line:84%
And they're applying
in an order.

00:48:33.040 --> 00:48:38.710 align:middle line:84%
And they don't necessarily
all get obeyed.

00:48:38.710 --> 00:48:41.510 align:middle line:90%
We did this on the board--

00:48:41.510 --> 00:48:42.010 align:middle line:90%
when was it?

00:48:42.010 --> 00:48:42.802 align:middle line:90%
Last time, I think.

00:48:42.802 --> 00:48:45.500 align:middle line:84%
But let's do it on the slide
a little more carefully.

00:48:45.500 --> 00:48:49.480 align:middle line:84%
So cast your minds back to when
we were talking about Lardil.

00:48:49.480 --> 00:48:51.790 align:middle line:84%
I tried to convince you--
we worked out an analysis

00:48:51.790 --> 00:48:57.760 align:middle line:84%
of Lardil in which, again,
bare nominative singular--

00:48:57.760 --> 00:48:58.990 align:middle line:90%
Lardil doesn't mark number.

00:48:58.990 --> 00:49:02.920 align:middle line:84%
But bare nominative
forms of nouns

00:49:02.920 --> 00:49:04.970 align:middle line:84%
undergo various kinds
of sound changes.

00:49:04.970 --> 00:49:08.200 align:middle line:84%
So Lardil has words
that underlyingly

00:49:08.200 --> 00:49:10.090 align:middle line:90%
end in a "k," for example.

00:49:10.090 --> 00:49:12.910 align:middle line:84%
And there was a
general rule in Lardil

00:49:12.910 --> 00:49:15.550 align:middle line:84%
that said if you
have a word that ends

00:49:15.550 --> 00:49:18.370 align:middle line:90%
in a "k," get rid of the "k."

00:49:18.370 --> 00:49:21.400 align:middle line:84%
So I'll start
writing these down.

00:49:21.400 --> 00:49:24.870 align:middle line:84%
Some Lardil rules-- one
of them was eliminate--

00:49:24.870 --> 00:49:26.530 align:middle line:84%
and I won't try to
write them formally.

00:49:26.530 --> 00:49:29.890 align:middle line:90%
Eliminate final "k."

00:49:29.890 --> 00:49:32.960 align:middle line:84%
So the word that we
think is underlyingly

00:49:32.960 --> 00:49:35.890 align:middle line:84%
"wangalk," the
word for boomerang,

00:49:35.890 --> 00:49:37.700 align:middle line:84%
in its nominative
form is "wangal."

00:49:37.700 --> 00:49:39.617 align:middle line:84%
And the reason we think
that it's underlyingly

00:49:39.617 --> 00:49:41.860 align:middle line:84%
"wangal" is that if you
add any suffixes to it,

00:49:41.860 --> 00:49:43.270 align:middle line:90%
you get to see that "k."

00:49:43.270 --> 00:49:47.380 align:middle line:84%
So the accusative, I think
I told you, was [LARDIL]..

00:49:47.380 --> 00:49:51.910 align:middle line:84%
So the "k" shows up, and
in other forms as well.

00:49:51.910 --> 00:49:54.790 align:middle line:84%
We thought there was
another rule that said

00:49:54.790 --> 00:50:00.460 align:middle line:90%
a final "u" becomes "a," yeah?

00:50:00.460 --> 00:50:03.430 align:middle line:84%
And that was why
"kandu," which is

00:50:03.430 --> 00:50:05.950 align:middle line:84%
the word for "blood"
underlyingly,

00:50:05.950 --> 00:50:08.530 align:middle line:84%
becomes "kanda"
in the nominative.

00:50:08.530 --> 00:50:10.720 align:middle line:84%
And in the accusative,
it's [LARDIL]..

00:50:10.720 --> 00:50:13.780 align:middle line:84%
That's why we think it
starts out as "kandu."

00:50:13.780 --> 00:50:15.602 align:middle line:90%
So we have these two rules.

00:50:15.602 --> 00:50:17.060 align:middle line:84%
And what we said
when we were doing

00:50:17.060 --> 00:50:20.440 align:middle line:84%
Lardil before was
something like we

00:50:20.440 --> 00:50:24.520 align:middle line:84%
might want these rules to
apply in a particular order.

00:50:24.520 --> 00:50:26.650 align:middle line:84%
In fact, I see that
I have written them

00:50:26.650 --> 00:50:28.750 align:middle line:90%
in the wrong order.

00:50:28.750 --> 00:50:32.770 align:middle line:84%
If you were to take the
word "ngaluk," which

00:50:32.770 --> 00:50:35.050 align:middle line:84%
is the underlying
form for "story,"

00:50:35.050 --> 00:50:38.510 align:middle line:84%
and apply this rule
and then this rule,

00:50:38.510 --> 00:50:40.660 align:middle line:84%
well then, you would
eliminate the final "k."

00:50:40.660 --> 00:50:42.070 align:middle line:90%
That would get you ngalu.

00:50:42.070 --> 00:50:44.080 align:middle line:84%
And then you would change
the final "u" to "a."

00:50:44.080 --> 00:50:46.090 align:middle line:84%
And then you would
get an "ngala."

00:50:46.090 --> 00:50:49.000 align:middle line:84%
But that's not, in fact, the
nominative form for "story."

00:50:49.000 --> 00:50:52.060 align:middle line:84%
The nominative form for
story is ngalu, yeah?

00:50:52.060 --> 00:50:55.640 align:middle line:84%
And so what I said at the
time was one way of thinking

00:50:55.640 --> 00:50:57.640 align:middle line:84%
about this, a popular way
of thinking about this

00:50:57.640 --> 00:50:59.560 align:middle line:84%
is, to think there
are these rules.

00:50:59.560 --> 00:51:02.470 align:middle line:90%
And it's like an assembly line.

00:51:02.470 --> 00:51:05.380 align:middle line:84%
The first station is the
one where you do this,

00:51:05.380 --> 00:51:07.150 align:middle line:90%
where you get rid of final "u."

00:51:07.150 --> 00:51:10.150 align:middle line:84%
And then after you're done
with that, you get handed off

00:51:10.150 --> 00:51:12.760 align:middle line:84%
to another place in
the assembly line

00:51:12.760 --> 00:51:15.340 align:middle line:90%
where they eliminate final "k."

00:51:15.340 --> 00:51:18.370 align:middle line:84%
And what's happened with that
"ngaluk," underlying "ngaluk,"

00:51:18.370 --> 00:51:20.560 align:middle line:84%
is that it got handed to
the first station first.

00:51:20.560 --> 00:51:22.690 align:middle line:84%
First station said, well,
this word, "ngaluk"--

00:51:22.690 --> 00:51:23.920 align:middle line:90%
it doesn't end in "u."

00:51:23.920 --> 00:51:25.390 align:middle line:90%
I'm not going to do anything.

00:51:25.390 --> 00:51:27.742 align:middle line:84%
And then it gets handed
to the second station.

00:51:27.742 --> 00:51:29.200 align:middle line:84%
The second station
says, oh, I need

00:51:29.200 --> 00:51:30.367 align:middle line:90%
to get rid of the final "k."

00:51:30.367 --> 00:51:32.198 align:middle line:84%
It'll be a "ngalu,"
by which point

00:51:32.198 --> 00:51:34.490 align:middle line:84%
the people in the first
station might have been saying,

00:51:34.490 --> 00:51:34.990 align:middle line:90%
wait, wait.

00:51:34.990 --> 00:51:35.930 align:middle line:90%
Let us have that back.

00:51:35.930 --> 00:51:37.900 align:middle line:90%
But it's too late, was the idea.

00:51:37.900 --> 00:51:40.390 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, so these are rules that
apply in a particular order.

00:51:40.390 --> 00:51:42.820 align:middle line:84%
That's the way we were
talking about Lardil.

00:51:42.820 --> 00:51:45.160 align:middle line:84%
Does that all sound
vaguely familiar?

00:51:45.160 --> 00:51:47.785 align:middle line:84%
There are these procedures, and
the procedures happen in order.

00:51:47.785 --> 00:51:50.530 align:middle line:90%


00:51:50.530 --> 00:51:52.740 align:middle line:84%
But if we imagine,
thinking about things

00:51:52.740 --> 00:51:55.080 align:middle line:84%
in an optimality
theoretic kind of way,

00:51:55.080 --> 00:51:59.670 align:middle line:84%
well, it's not hard to come up
with constraints that say this.

00:51:59.670 --> 00:52:04.980 align:middle line:84%
Star, final-- I have to
write it in capital letters

00:52:04.980 --> 00:52:06.990 align:middle line:84%
because that's what you
do in optimality theory.

00:52:06.990 --> 00:52:08.550 align:middle line:90%
No final "k."

00:52:08.550 --> 00:52:13.170 align:middle line:90%
No final "u," yeah?

00:52:13.170 --> 00:52:15.550 align:middle line:84%
And those constraints
would look at "ngaluk"--

00:52:15.550 --> 00:52:17.620 align:middle line:84%
or sorry, "wangalk,"
and they would say,

00:52:17.620 --> 00:52:18.540 align:middle line:90%
no, we can't have the final "k."

00:52:18.540 --> 00:52:19.290 align:middle line:90%
It'll be one "wangal."

00:52:19.290 --> 00:52:20.582 align:middle line:90%
And they would look at "kandu."

00:52:20.582 --> 00:52:23.220 align:middle line:84%
And they would say, no,
can't have the final "u."

00:52:23.220 --> 00:52:24.780 align:middle line:90%
It's got to be "kanda."

00:52:24.780 --> 00:52:26.280 align:middle line:84%
It might have other
constraints that

00:52:26.280 --> 00:52:29.520 align:middle line:84%
are responsible for helping
you decide the best thing to do

00:52:29.520 --> 00:52:32.700 align:middle line:84%
with "kandu" is to change it
to "kanda" instead of "kandi,"

00:52:32.700 --> 00:52:34.897 align:middle line:84%
or "kand," or any of a
variety of other things

00:52:34.897 --> 00:52:35.730 align:middle line:90%
you could have done.

00:52:35.730 --> 00:52:37.380 align:middle line:84%
So it's not all
that hard to have

00:52:37.380 --> 00:52:39.960 align:middle line:84%
constraints that say there's
something wrong with "wangalk."

00:52:39.960 --> 00:52:43.640 align:middle line:84%
There's something
wrong with "kandu."

00:52:43.640 --> 00:52:47.400 align:middle line:84%
But it's not easy to
figure out what to do next.

00:52:47.400 --> 00:52:50.900 align:middle line:84%
So if you have those conditions
that say Lardil cannot stand

00:52:50.900 --> 00:52:55.400 align:middle line:84%
words that end in "k."
or words that end in "u,"

00:52:55.400 --> 00:52:58.310 align:middle line:84%
that's the kind of thing we were
saying about Japanese accent.

00:52:58.310 --> 00:53:02.360 align:middle line:84%
Japanese doesn't like
accent on the last syllable.

00:53:02.360 --> 00:53:05.200 align:middle line:84%
So avoid it if you're
making a compound.

00:53:05.200 --> 00:53:07.900 align:middle line:84%
Those generalizations hold for
"boomerang" and for "blood."

00:53:07.900 --> 00:53:09.550 align:middle line:90%
But they don't hold for "story."

00:53:09.550 --> 00:53:12.850 align:middle line:84%
The one about not having a word
ending in "u" doesn't hold.

00:53:12.850 --> 00:53:13.983 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:53:13.983 --> 00:53:16.150 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: You talked about
no final "k," no final "u"

00:53:16.150 --> 00:53:17.813 align:middle line:84%
as being part of
optimality theory.

00:53:17.813 --> 00:53:18.730 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:53:18.730 --> 00:53:21.340 align:middle line:90%


00:53:21.340 --> 00:53:26.030 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Is it possible
to satisfy both

00:53:26.030 --> 00:53:28.978 align:middle line:90%
if you were to do "ngala"?

00:53:28.978 --> 00:53:31.070 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Mm-hmm.

00:53:31.070 --> 00:53:36.692 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Does optimality theory
support each of the rules?

00:53:36.692 --> 00:53:38.150 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
So that's just it.

00:53:38.150 --> 00:53:38.900 align:middle line:90%
It doesn't, right?

00:53:38.900 --> 00:53:41.005 align:middle line:84%
So the conditions--
the sense in which

00:53:41.005 --> 00:53:42.380 align:middle line:84%
it has something
like an order is

00:53:42.380 --> 00:53:44.630 align:middle line:84%
that the conditions are ranked
with respect to each other.

00:53:44.630 --> 00:53:46.963 align:middle line:84%
There's the one that's most
important and the one that's

00:53:46.963 --> 00:53:49.320 align:middle line:90%
next most important, and so on.

00:53:49.320 --> 00:53:52.910 align:middle line:84%
But if we were to
build a tableau,

00:53:52.910 --> 00:53:56.360 align:middle line:84%
we would have these
two conditions.

00:53:56.360 --> 00:53:58.910 align:middle line:90%
Don't have final "k."

00:53:58.910 --> 00:54:04.370 align:middle line:90%
Don't have final "u."

00:54:04.370 --> 00:54:07.850 align:middle line:84%
And we would start
off with "ngaluk."

00:54:07.850 --> 00:54:10.580 align:middle line:84%
And we'd consider
keeping "ngaluk."

00:54:10.580 --> 00:54:15.410 align:middle line:84%
And we'd consider getting
rid of the "k" at the end.

00:54:15.410 --> 00:54:18.470 align:middle line:84%
And maybe we'd consider
"ngala," right?

00:54:18.470 --> 00:54:23.030 align:middle line:84%
And this one would get knocked
out by star final "k," right?

00:54:23.030 --> 00:54:24.470 align:middle line:90%
Because it would end in "k."

00:54:24.470 --> 00:54:28.520 align:middle line:84%
And this one would get knocked
out by star final "u," yeah?

00:54:28.520 --> 00:54:31.800 align:middle line:84%
And this one would pass
both of those conditions.

00:54:31.800 --> 00:54:34.550 align:middle line:84%
So if we just did a tableau
like this, if we said,

00:54:34.550 --> 00:54:36.710 align:middle line:84%
Lardil seems to not like
this and not like that,

00:54:36.710 --> 00:54:40.140 align:middle line:84%
and we have evidence for
that in these other cases,

00:54:40.140 --> 00:54:43.070 align:middle line:84%
it seems as though "ngalu"
ought to become "ngala."

00:54:43.070 --> 00:54:44.480 align:middle line:90%
But that's wrong.

00:54:44.480 --> 00:54:47.640 align:middle line:84%
So the fact is this is
the one we want to win.

00:54:47.640 --> 00:54:52.550 align:middle line:84%
And it's hard to see, once we've
posited these conditions, how

00:54:52.550 --> 00:54:54.140 align:middle line:90%
we can get this to win.

00:54:54.140 --> 00:54:57.230 align:middle line:84%
This seems like it ought
to be better once we've

00:54:57.230 --> 00:54:58.500 align:middle line:90%
posited these conditions.

00:54:58.500 --> 00:55:00.667 align:middle line:84%
So the puzzle-- and there's
something people work on

00:55:00.667 --> 00:55:03.170 align:middle line:84%
is try to figure out,
why is that second one

00:55:03.170 --> 00:55:04.760 align:middle line:90%
better than the third one?

00:55:04.760 --> 00:55:05.420 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:55:05.420 --> 00:55:07.823 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: How about we rephrase
the no final "u" rule?

00:55:07.823 --> 00:55:08.740 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:55:08.740 --> 00:55:11.530 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: We rephrase it as
no final "u" unless it's

00:55:11.530 --> 00:55:13.090 align:middle line:90%
original form ends with a "k."

00:55:13.090 --> 00:55:14.090 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Ah, OK.

00:55:14.090 --> 00:55:15.710 align:middle line:84%
So we could have
a rule that says--

00:55:15.710 --> 00:55:16.700 align:middle line:90%
that's nice.

00:55:16.700 --> 00:55:19.380 align:middle line:90%
We could say, no final "u."

00:55:19.380 --> 00:55:21.950 align:middle line:84%
This is another way of
saying what you just said.

00:55:21.950 --> 00:55:28.955 align:middle line:84%
No final "u" that was
final in original form.

00:55:28.955 --> 00:55:32.080 align:middle line:90%


00:55:32.080 --> 00:55:32.710 align:middle line:90%
Yep.

00:55:32.710 --> 00:55:35.830 align:middle line:84%
So we need more capital
letters, but it's OK.

00:55:35.830 --> 00:55:42.700 align:middle line:84%
So yes, if we state
it that way, then this

00:55:42.700 --> 00:55:45.290 align:middle line:90%
passes that version of it.

00:55:45.290 --> 00:55:47.650 align:middle line:84%
And as long as we have some
other condition that says,

00:55:47.650 --> 00:55:49.540 align:middle line:84%
don't change vowels
just for the fun of it,

00:55:49.540 --> 00:55:53.840 align:middle line:84%
don't change "u" because you
want to, which we surely need,

00:55:53.840 --> 00:55:54.340 align:middle line:90%
right?

00:55:54.340 --> 00:55:57.430 align:middle line:84%
If you give Lardil
a word that has

00:55:57.430 --> 00:56:01.540 align:middle line:84%
an "u" in it that's not at the
end, it's not going to change.

00:56:01.540 --> 00:56:03.790 align:middle line:90%
Then yes, then this would win.

00:56:03.790 --> 00:56:04.630 align:middle line:90%
That's right.

00:56:04.630 --> 00:56:07.720 align:middle line:84%
That is indeed one of the kinds
of things people do in order

00:56:07.720 --> 00:56:09.040 align:middle line:90%
to handle this kind of problem.

00:56:09.040 --> 00:56:14.650 align:middle line:84%
They make these conditions
more complicated because now

00:56:14.650 --> 00:56:18.820 align:middle line:84%
the conditions we've had so
far are all about the form that

00:56:18.820 --> 00:56:19.880 align:middle line:90%
gets pronounced.

00:56:19.880 --> 00:56:22.240 align:middle line:84%
They say things like the
form that gets pronounced

00:56:22.240 --> 00:56:24.310 align:middle line:84%
had better not end in
a "k," or the form that

00:56:24.310 --> 00:56:26.680 align:middle line:84%
gets pronounced had better
not end in a voiced obstruent

00:56:26.680 --> 00:56:29.502 align:middle line:84%
if we're doing Polish,
or it'd better not

00:56:29.502 --> 00:56:31.210 align:middle line:84%
have two stridents at
the end of the word

00:56:31.210 --> 00:56:32.807 align:middle line:90%
in a language like English.

00:56:32.807 --> 00:56:34.390 align:middle line:84%
This is a more
complicated constraint.

00:56:34.390 --> 00:56:38.050 align:middle line:84%
It says it had better not
end in this kind of sound

00:56:38.050 --> 00:56:40.600 align:middle line:84%
if it ended in
this kind of sound

00:56:40.600 --> 00:56:44.050 align:middle line:90%
originally, before we started.

00:56:44.050 --> 00:56:48.430 align:middle line:84%
So there's an actual debate
going on in optimality theory

00:56:48.430 --> 00:56:51.202 align:middle line:84%
about whether we want to allow
ourselves that power, allowing

00:56:51.202 --> 00:56:52.660 align:middle line:84%
constraints that
make reference not

00:56:52.660 --> 00:56:55.213 align:middle line:84%
just to the pronounced version
but to an earlier version.

00:56:55.213 --> 00:56:56.380 align:middle line:90%
But you're absolutely right.

00:56:56.380 --> 00:57:00.160 align:middle line:84%
That's a move people
make, people argue for.

00:57:00.160 --> 00:57:01.990 align:middle line:84%
I'm bringing this
up just to show you

00:57:01.990 --> 00:57:04.000 align:middle line:84%
there's this tension
between these two ways

00:57:04.000 --> 00:57:06.070 align:middle line:90%
of thinking about phonology.

00:57:06.070 --> 00:57:09.130 align:middle line:84%
And you've just raised one
of the ways, one of the ways

00:57:09.130 --> 00:57:12.070 align:middle line:84%
that, indeed, has been
taken in the literature

00:57:12.070 --> 00:57:15.230 align:middle line:84%
to try to figure out how
to deal with this tension.

00:57:15.230 --> 00:57:17.290 align:middle line:84%
But if you thought
that linguistics

00:57:17.290 --> 00:57:20.290 align:middle line:84%
was finished, like we
understood everything, no.

00:57:20.290 --> 00:57:22.540 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, so this is one
of the kinds of things

00:57:22.540 --> 00:57:24.760 align:middle line:84%
that linguists
still fight about--

00:57:24.760 --> 00:57:26.680 align:middle line:84%
how to deal with these
kinds of tensions.

00:57:26.680 --> 00:57:28.470 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:57:28.470 --> 00:57:31.040 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: I think we can still
think of it as an assembly

00:57:31.040 --> 00:57:31.540 align:middle line:90%
line.

00:57:31.540 --> 00:57:32.457 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:57:32.457 --> 00:57:34.870 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: If we think of
it as an assembly line,

00:57:34.870 --> 00:57:38.780 align:middle line:84%
and we throw at that, we can
make it like a priority then.

00:57:38.780 --> 00:57:39.780 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Uh-huh.

00:57:39.780 --> 00:57:42.500 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: We can make it
like an assembly language.

00:57:42.500 --> 00:57:43.500 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Uh-huh.

00:57:43.500 --> 00:57:44.935 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: We can form rules.

00:57:44.935 --> 00:57:45.810 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yes.

00:57:45.810 --> 00:57:47.583 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Actually
enforce the priority.

00:57:47.583 --> 00:57:48.750 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah, yeah.

00:57:48.750 --> 00:57:51.870 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, so that is another
move that people make.

00:57:51.870 --> 00:57:53.820 align:middle line:90%
Sorry, this slide has gone away.

00:57:53.820 --> 00:57:57.980 align:middle line:90%


00:57:57.980 --> 00:58:02.130 align:middle line:84%
That is another move that
people make, where they say,

00:58:02.130 --> 00:58:04.820 align:middle line:84%
well, there are various ways
of interpreting what you just

00:58:04.820 --> 00:58:05.840 align:middle line:90%
said.

00:58:05.840 --> 00:58:10.172 align:middle line:84%
One move that people made,
especially early on when people

00:58:10.172 --> 00:58:12.380 align:middle line:84%
were worried about this
problem in optimality theory,

00:58:12.380 --> 00:58:15.860 align:middle line:84%
was to say we're
going to do something

00:58:15.860 --> 00:58:18.770 align:middle line:84%
in optimality theory that
allows us to simulate

00:58:18.770 --> 00:58:20.510 align:middle line:90%
having rules in an order.

00:58:20.510 --> 00:58:24.830 align:middle line:84%
So what we'll do is we'll have
a word go through the tableaux

00:58:24.830 --> 00:58:26.600 align:middle line:90%
more than once.

00:58:26.600 --> 00:58:31.010 align:middle line:84%
Or the conditions can
be in different orders

00:58:31.010 --> 00:58:32.300 align:middle line:90%
in the different tableaux.

00:58:32.300 --> 00:58:35.210 align:middle line:84%
So we'll have a
tableau first that

00:58:35.210 --> 00:58:39.440 align:middle line:84%
says final "k" is
very bad, let's say.

00:58:39.440 --> 00:58:43.280 align:middle line:84%
And it doesn't have
final "u" is bad, right?

00:58:43.280 --> 00:58:44.780 align:middle line:84%
And then we'll have
another tableau

00:58:44.780 --> 00:58:46.850 align:middle line:84%
that maybe doesn't have
final "k" is very bad,

00:58:46.850 --> 00:58:49.340 align:middle line:90%
but has final "u" is bad, right?

00:58:49.340 --> 00:58:52.820 align:middle line:84%
And that's a way to
simulate ordered rules,

00:58:52.820 --> 00:58:54.530 align:middle line:84%
basically, is to
say we go through

00:58:54.530 --> 00:58:56.120 align:middle line:90%
tableaux multiple times.

00:58:56.120 --> 00:58:58.215 align:middle line:84%
And the tableaux look
different from each other.

00:58:58.215 --> 00:58:59.840 align:middle line:84%
They have different
conditions in them,

00:58:59.840 --> 00:59:01.840 align:middle line:84%
or they have the conditions
in different orders.

00:59:01.840 --> 00:59:03.720 align:middle line:90%
That's one way to talk about it.

00:59:03.720 --> 00:59:06.000 align:middle line:84%
You might have had a
more clever idea, though,

00:59:06.000 --> 00:59:08.120 align:middle line:90%
which is the tableaux--

00:59:08.120 --> 00:59:12.862 align:middle line:84%
I was talking as though these
rules are ranked with respect

00:59:12.862 --> 00:59:13.445 align:middle line:90%
to each other.

00:59:13.445 --> 00:59:15.028 align:middle line:84%
You know which ones
are more important

00:59:15.028 --> 00:59:15.950 align:middle line:90%
than which other ones.

00:59:15.950 --> 00:59:18.170 align:middle line:84%
But optimality theory--
people are generally

00:59:18.170 --> 00:59:22.820 align:middle line:84%
talking not as though
those happen one at a time.

00:59:22.820 --> 00:59:25.760 align:middle line:84%
It's just you're considering
all these possibilities.

00:59:25.760 --> 00:59:28.887 align:middle line:84%
And you decide which one is
best by all of these conditions.

00:59:28.887 --> 00:59:31.220 align:middle line:84%
You sound like you're thinking
about something different

00:59:31.220 --> 00:59:34.880 align:middle line:84%
where you would say you'd
go back to the simple one

00:59:34.880 --> 00:59:36.980 align:middle line:84%
and you'd say, we
start with "ngaluk."

00:59:36.980 --> 00:59:39.860 align:middle line:90%


00:59:39.860 --> 00:59:43.700 align:middle line:84%
And what we want is something
that says something like,

00:59:43.700 --> 00:59:47.510 align:middle line:84%
well, I should have put
star final "u" first.

00:59:47.510 --> 00:59:49.145 align:middle line:90%
I'll just reverse these.

00:59:49.145 --> 00:59:54.160 align:middle line:90%


00:59:54.160 --> 00:59:57.010 align:middle line:84%
We have all of our
candidates here.

00:59:57.010 --> 00:59:59.930 align:middle line:84%
And we first consider
a star final "u."

00:59:59.930 --> 01:00:00.430 align:middle line:90%
Uh-oh.

01:00:00.430 --> 01:00:02.200 align:middle line:84%
But if we consider
a star final "u,"

01:00:02.200 --> 01:00:03.700 align:middle line:84%
star final "u" will
look at "ngalu,"

01:00:03.700 --> 01:00:06.070 align:middle line:90%
and it will knock it out, right?

01:00:06.070 --> 01:00:08.320 align:middle line:90%
So that will be bad right there.

01:00:08.320 --> 01:00:11.655 align:middle line:84%
And we won't ever get
it back if we do that.

01:00:11.655 --> 01:00:13.030 align:middle line:84%
So what we want
is something more

01:00:13.030 --> 01:00:15.730 align:middle line:84%
like using the tableaux
to do something

01:00:15.730 --> 01:00:19.240 align:middle line:84%
like ordered rules,
something that says

01:00:19.240 --> 01:00:20.440 align:middle line:90%
we look at this first thing.

01:00:20.440 --> 01:00:25.218 align:middle line:84%
And we are now basically
doing rules in an order.

01:00:25.218 --> 01:00:27.260 align:middle line:84%
We look at this first
thing and we ask ourselves,

01:00:27.260 --> 01:00:27.927 align:middle line:90%
what's "ngaluk"?

01:00:27.927 --> 01:00:31.370 align:middle line:84%
Well, it doesn't
violate this first rule.

01:00:31.370 --> 01:00:32.950 align:middle line:90%
And so we'll keep it.

01:00:32.950 --> 01:00:35.290 align:middle line:84%
And then we'll
consider the next rule.

01:00:35.290 --> 01:00:36.470 align:middle line:90%
It does violate that rule.

01:00:36.470 --> 01:00:37.428 align:middle line:90%
And so we'll change it.

01:00:37.428 --> 01:00:38.980 align:middle line:84%
We'll make a minimal
change to it.

01:00:38.980 --> 01:00:41.780 align:middle line:84%
This is a way of converting
tableaux into ordered rules,

01:00:41.780 --> 01:00:42.280 align:middle line:90%
I think.

01:00:42.280 --> 01:00:44.590 align:middle line:90%
That's what we're now doing.

01:00:44.590 --> 01:00:47.415 align:middle line:90%
And it could be.

01:00:47.415 --> 01:00:49.540 align:middle line:84%
These are all things to
think about-- open research

01:00:49.540 --> 01:00:51.320 align:middle line:90%
questions.

01:00:51.320 --> 01:00:52.981 align:middle line:90%
Cool.

01:00:52.981 --> 01:00:55.180 align:middle line:84%
OK, so the goal here
was to get all of you

01:00:55.180 --> 01:00:57.800 align:middle line:84%
to try to do better than
linguistics has so far done.

01:00:57.800 --> 01:00:59.290 align:middle line:84%
So right now we
have these two ways

01:00:59.290 --> 01:01:00.520 align:middle line:90%
of thinking about phonology.

01:01:00.520 --> 01:01:02.830 align:middle line:84%
And there are in some kind
of conflict with each other.

01:01:02.830 --> 01:01:04.788 align:middle line:84%
And there's active work
on trying to figure out

01:01:04.788 --> 01:01:05.980 align:middle line:90%
how to get them to mesh.

01:01:05.980 --> 01:01:10.740 align:middle line:90%


01:01:10.740 --> 01:01:12.090 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:01:12.090 --> 01:01:13.590 align:middle line:90%
Cool.

01:01:13.590 --> 01:01:14.160 align:middle line:90%
Oops.

01:01:14.160 --> 01:01:16.320 align:middle line:90%
Where are we?

01:01:16.320 --> 01:01:17.415 align:middle line:90%
Here we are.

01:01:17.415 --> 01:01:19.450 align:middle line:84%
Sonorants and
obstruents, we did that.

01:01:19.450 --> 01:01:20.683 align:middle line:90%
We did that.

01:01:20.683 --> 01:01:24.330 align:middle line:90%
OK, so let me introduce you--

01:01:24.330 --> 01:01:25.950 align:middle line:84%
we're running--
yeah, yeah, yeah.

01:01:25.950 --> 01:01:32.100 align:middle line:84%
Let me introduce you to
another class of sounds.

01:01:32.100 --> 01:01:36.720 align:middle line:84%
Again, the point is to
show you ways in which it's

01:01:36.720 --> 01:01:38.460 align:middle line:90%
useful to categorize sounds.

01:01:38.460 --> 01:01:40.290 align:middle line:84%
And the way to try to
convince you of that

01:01:40.290 --> 01:01:44.430 align:middle line:84%
is to show you phenomena that
care about certain classes

01:01:44.430 --> 01:01:46.410 align:middle line:90%
of sounds and not others.

01:01:46.410 --> 01:01:49.200 align:middle line:84%
Here are a bunch of
Arabic nouns together

01:01:49.200 --> 01:01:52.980 align:middle line:84%
with their definite article,
which you can see is "al."

01:01:52.980 --> 01:01:55.650 align:middle line:84%
English has borrowed a
bunch of Arabic words

01:01:55.650 --> 01:01:56.820 align:middle line:90%
that have kept this "al."

01:01:56.820 --> 01:01:59.880 align:middle line:84%
So we have words like "algebra,"
for example, or "algorithm,"

01:01:59.880 --> 01:02:04.530 align:middle line:84%
where the "al-" at the end
is this word for "the."

01:02:04.530 --> 01:02:06.750 align:middle line:84%
So here are some Arabic
nouns with the word for "the"

01:02:06.750 --> 01:02:08.370 align:middle line:90%
in front of them.

01:02:08.370 --> 01:02:12.990 align:middle line:84%
But there's this other class of
nouns, where the word for "the"

01:02:12.990 --> 01:02:13.720 align:middle line:90%
changes.

01:02:13.720 --> 01:02:15.930 align:middle line:90%
It's no longer "al."

01:02:15.930 --> 01:02:18.360 align:middle line:84%
What it gets-- it
still starts with "a,"

01:02:18.360 --> 01:02:22.260 align:middle line:84%
but the consonant in it becomes
a copy of the consonant that's

01:02:22.260 --> 01:02:23.680 align:middle line:90%
after it.

01:02:23.680 --> 01:02:26.790 align:middle line:84%
So the word for sun,
which is [ARABIC]..

01:02:26.790 --> 01:02:28.380 align:middle line:90%
The sun is [ARABIC].

01:02:28.380 --> 01:02:30.288 align:middle line:90%
It's not "al-" [ARABIC].

01:02:30.288 --> 01:02:31.830 align:middle line:84%
Here I am pretending
to speak Arabic.

01:02:31.830 --> 01:02:35.280 align:middle line:84%
Is there anyone here who
actually speaks Arabic?

01:02:35.280 --> 01:02:35.780 align:middle line:90%
OK, cool.

01:02:35.780 --> 01:02:38.624 align:middle line:90%
I'll just keep pretending, then.

01:02:38.624 --> 01:02:39.930 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:02:39.930 --> 01:02:41.180 align:middle line:90%
This is a classic observation.

01:02:41.180 --> 01:02:45.110 align:middle line:84%
Arabic linguists noticed
this about their language.

01:02:45.110 --> 01:02:48.533 align:middle line:84%
They name and noticed
the relevant condition

01:02:48.533 --> 01:02:50.450 align:middle line:84%
is that there are certain
kinds of sounds that

01:02:50.450 --> 01:02:53.960 align:middle line:84%
get the phenomenon on the right,
where the definite article ends

01:02:53.960 --> 01:02:58.220 align:middle line:84%
in a copy of the first
consonant of the following noun,

01:02:58.220 --> 01:03:00.020 align:middle line:84%
and other consonants
that don't do that.

01:03:00.020 --> 01:03:03.170 align:middle line:84%
They're called "moon
letters" and "sun letters"

01:03:03.170 --> 01:03:06.140 align:middle line:84%
in the Arabic
tradition because well,

01:03:06.140 --> 01:03:08.745 align:middle line:84%
"moon" is one of the words
in the column on the left.

01:03:08.745 --> 01:03:11.120 align:middle line:84%
And "sun" is one of the words
in the column on the right.

01:03:11.120 --> 01:03:14.700 align:middle line:84%
It's a mnemonic for
these classes of sounds.

01:03:14.700 --> 01:03:17.870 align:middle line:84%
So the moon letters are
letters like "kuh," and "fuh,"

01:03:17.870 --> 01:03:21.900 align:middle line:84%
and "k," and "h,"
and glottal stop.

01:03:21.900 --> 01:03:25.740 align:middle line:84%
And the sun letters are sounds
like "sh," "d," "z," "n,"

01:03:25.740 --> 01:03:26.470 align:middle line:90%
and "th."

01:03:26.470 --> 01:03:32.820 align:middle line:90%


01:03:32.820 --> 01:03:34.230 align:middle line:90%
The sun letters-- yes.

01:03:34.230 --> 01:03:38.550 align:middle line:84%
The sun letters are the ones
that I've got in the box there.

01:03:38.550 --> 01:03:41.010 align:middle line:84%
They are what are
called coronals.

01:03:41.010 --> 01:03:44.100 align:middle line:84%
So coronals are the
interdentals and alveolars

01:03:44.100 --> 01:03:45.970 align:middle line:90%
and the postalveolars.

01:03:45.970 --> 01:03:47.500 align:middle line:90%
If you think about them--

01:03:47.500 --> 01:03:51.000 align:middle line:84%
so feel free to sit there
making those noises to yourself

01:03:51.000 --> 01:03:53.210 align:middle line:90%
if you want to--

01:03:53.210 --> 01:03:57.150 align:middle line:84%
what all of them do is make
a consonant sound that's

01:03:57.150 --> 01:03:59.740 align:middle line:84%
articulated with the tip or
the blade of your tongue--

01:03:59.740 --> 01:04:01.350 align:middle line:84%
so if the front
of your tongue is

01:04:01.350 --> 01:04:05.830 align:middle line:84%
touching some part of the top
of your mouth or other places.

01:04:05.830 --> 01:04:07.380 align:middle line:84%
So the interdentals--
your tongue

01:04:07.380 --> 01:04:09.810 align:middle line:84%
is sticking out between your
teeth, or the alveolars,

01:04:09.810 --> 01:04:12.480 align:middle line:84%
or the postalveolars--
the tip of your tongue

01:04:12.480 --> 01:04:15.750 align:middle line:84%
or the blade of your tongue
is coming into contact almost

01:04:15.750 --> 01:04:20.225 align:middle line:84%
with the top of your mouth as
opposed to, say, bilabials,

01:04:20.225 --> 01:04:22.350 align:middle line:84%
where your tongue can do
whatever the heck it feels

01:04:22.350 --> 01:04:27.420 align:middle line:84%
like, or velars, which involve
your tongue, or uvulars,

01:04:27.420 --> 01:04:28.560 align:middle line:90%
which involve your tongue.

01:04:28.560 --> 01:04:31.020 align:middle line:84%
But it's the back
of your tongue, "k,"

01:04:31.020 --> 01:04:33.258 align:middle line:84%
or "g," as opposed to the
front of your tongue, which

01:04:33.258 --> 01:04:34.966 align:middle line:84%
is what you're using
for things like "t,"

01:04:34.966 --> 01:04:38.790 align:middle line:84%
and "d," and "f," and
"th" and "sh" and "zh."

01:04:38.790 --> 01:04:39.660 align:middle line:90%
Is that clear?

01:04:39.660 --> 01:04:41.340 align:middle line:90%
That distinction clear?

01:04:41.340 --> 01:04:47.340 align:middle line:84%
And Arabic gives this a
nice illustration of this.

01:04:47.340 --> 01:04:50.430 align:middle line:84%
The coronal sounds are the
ones that are the sun letters.

01:04:50.430 --> 01:04:54.300 align:middle line:84%
They're the ones where
the last consonant

01:04:54.300 --> 01:04:56.400 align:middle line:84%
of the definite article
just becomes a copy

01:04:56.400 --> 01:04:58.170 align:middle line:90%
of the following consonant.

01:04:58.170 --> 01:05:00.780 align:middle line:84%
Notice that the last consonant
of the indefinite article

01:05:00.780 --> 01:05:04.380 align:middle line:84%
underlyingly is "l,"
which is itself coronal.

01:05:04.380 --> 01:05:08.070 align:middle line:84%
So it's like the end
of the definite article

01:05:08.070 --> 01:05:09.630 align:middle line:90%
is going to be coronal.

01:05:09.630 --> 01:05:11.983 align:middle line:84%
If nothing else happens,
it's going to be "l,"

01:05:11.983 --> 01:05:13.650 align:middle line:84%
But if there's a
coronal right after it,

01:05:13.650 --> 01:05:15.930 align:middle line:84%
it just becomes a copy
of the following coronal.

01:05:15.930 --> 01:05:17.730 align:middle line:90%
Does that make sense?

01:05:17.730 --> 01:05:20.440 align:middle line:84%
That's what's going on
in all of these examples.

01:05:20.440 --> 01:05:22.560 align:middle line:84%
So with "moon," and "mare,"
and "book," and "war,"

01:05:22.560 --> 01:05:25.650 align:middle line:84%
and "father," the "l"
of the definite article

01:05:25.650 --> 01:05:28.290 align:middle line:84%
can't become a copy of
the following consonant

01:05:28.290 --> 01:05:32.370 align:middle line:84%
and remain coronal,
whereas in the sun letters,

01:05:32.370 --> 01:05:34.230 align:middle line:84%
the ones on the
right, by copying

01:05:34.230 --> 01:05:37.470 align:middle line:84%
the following consonant, the
"l" of the definite article

01:05:37.470 --> 01:05:40.260 align:middle line:90%
is still a coronal.

01:05:40.260 --> 01:05:41.010 align:middle line:90%
[ELECTRONIC CHIME]

01:05:41.010 --> 01:05:42.450 align:middle line:90%
Bling.

01:05:42.450 --> 01:05:44.040 align:middle line:84%
Somebody had a great
idea about that.

01:05:44.040 --> 01:05:46.920 align:middle line:90%


01:05:46.920 --> 01:05:49.775 align:middle line:84%
So that's what's going on
in these Arabic examples.

01:05:49.775 --> 01:05:52.802 align:middle line:90%


01:05:52.802 --> 01:05:55.260 align:middle line:84%
A rule that we could have for
the Arabic definite article--

01:05:55.260 --> 01:05:56.070 align:middle line:90%
yes, I just said this.

01:05:56.070 --> 01:05:56.570 align:middle line:90%
Thank you.

01:05:56.570 --> 01:05:58.013 align:middle line:90%
"l" is also coronal.

01:05:58.013 --> 01:06:00.180 align:middle line:84%
A rule we could have for
the Arabic definite article

01:06:00.180 --> 01:06:02.400 align:middle line:84%
would say in the
definite article,

01:06:02.400 --> 01:06:06.610 align:middle line:84%
an "l" becomes a copy of a
following coronal consonant.

01:06:06.610 --> 01:06:10.710 align:middle line:84%
So we have a variable,
consonant subscript "i"

01:06:10.710 --> 01:06:13.920 align:middle line:84%
which says whatever the
following consonant is if it's

01:06:13.920 --> 01:06:19.200 align:middle line:84%
coronal, the "l' will also
get the value consonant sub i,

01:06:19.200 --> 01:06:22.510 align:middle line:84%
but it'll have the same form
as the following consonant.

01:06:22.510 --> 01:06:23.010 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:06:23.010 --> 01:06:24.750 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: I had a
question about coronals.

01:06:24.750 --> 01:06:28.982 align:middle line:84%
So what I noticed
is the sun letters

01:06:28.982 --> 01:06:31.232 align:middle line:84%
are the letters that you can
maintain for a long time,

01:06:31.232 --> 01:06:35.760 align:middle line:84%
like "sh," and like the
"sh," like [ARABIC] in sun,

01:06:35.760 --> 01:06:38.013 align:middle line:90%
for "d" in [ARABIC].

01:06:38.013 --> 01:06:38.930 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

01:06:38.930 --> 01:06:40.580 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: You maintain
them for a long time.

01:06:40.580 --> 01:06:41.497 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

01:06:41.497 --> 01:06:44.210 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: And Arabic
actually has [ARABIC],,

01:06:44.210 --> 01:06:46.383 align:middle line:90%
which duplicates one letter.

01:06:46.383 --> 01:06:47.300 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

01:06:47.300 --> 01:06:49.610 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: So what I'm
thinking is that when

01:06:49.610 --> 01:06:51.885 align:middle line:84%
you can duplicate the letter,
you just duplicate it.

01:06:51.885 --> 01:06:52.300 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Uh-huh.

01:06:52.300 --> 01:06:54.110 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: With [ARABIC],,
and other words.

01:06:54.110 --> 01:06:55.250 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

01:06:55.250 --> 01:07:00.320 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: But for [ARABIC],, just
the moon, you can't do that.

01:07:00.320 --> 01:07:01.370 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

01:07:01.370 --> 01:07:02.510 align:middle line:90%
But there are--

01:07:02.510 --> 01:07:03.290 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Do you see what
I'm saying with that?

01:07:03.290 --> 01:07:04.832 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: I
see what you mean.

01:07:04.832 --> 01:07:05.360 align:middle line:90%
But look.

01:07:05.360 --> 01:07:12.230 align:middle line:84%
There are consonants like "p,"
and "t," and "k," which are all

01:07:12.230 --> 01:07:13.190 align:middle line:90%
stops.

01:07:13.190 --> 01:07:17.450 align:middle line:84%
But "t" is coronal and "p"
and "k" are not, right?

01:07:17.450 --> 01:07:20.870 align:middle line:84%
And so all of those are
equally hard to maintain

01:07:20.870 --> 01:07:22.190 align:middle line:90%
for a long time, I think.

01:07:22.190 --> 01:07:24.140 align:middle line:84%
Or similarly, I think
what we're hoping--

01:07:24.140 --> 01:07:26.270 align:middle line:84%
and you can stop me
if this is not true--

01:07:26.270 --> 01:07:31.160 align:middle line:84%
is that if there are Arabic
words that start with "m,"

01:07:31.160 --> 01:07:35.210 align:middle line:84%
you'll get al
before an "m," yeah?

01:07:35.210 --> 01:07:37.550 align:middle line:84%
But for Arabic words
that start with "n,"

01:07:37.550 --> 01:07:39.770 align:middle line:90%
you get a copy of the "n," yeah?

01:07:39.770 --> 01:07:43.340 align:middle line:84%
And again, those are all
equally easy to hold on

01:07:43.340 --> 01:07:44.360 align:middle line:90%
for a long time.

01:07:44.360 --> 01:07:46.220 align:middle line:84%
So this is a good
example of science.

01:07:46.220 --> 01:07:47.610 align:middle line:90%
You had a good hypothesis.

01:07:47.610 --> 01:07:49.760 align:middle line:84%
And now we're
finding data, yeah.

01:07:49.760 --> 01:07:51.530 align:middle line:84%
And I should have
called on you to do

01:07:51.530 --> 01:07:53.390 align:middle line:90%
to say these Arabic words.

01:07:53.390 --> 01:07:54.623 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, Joseph?

01:07:54.623 --> 01:07:57.762 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: So you mentioned
that the English language

01:07:57.762 --> 01:08:00.013 align:middle line:84%
has borrowed quite a
few words from Arabic.

01:08:00.013 --> 01:08:00.930 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

01:08:00.930 --> 01:08:04.980 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: And we accidentally
brought the definite article

01:08:04.980 --> 01:08:05.870 align:middle line:90%
with it.

01:08:05.870 --> 01:08:06.970 align:middle line:90%
So the word "algebra."

01:08:06.970 --> 01:08:08.866 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yep.

01:08:08.866 --> 01:08:10.282 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: "Algebra."

01:08:10.282 --> 01:08:12.642 align:middle line:84%
At least, the
English pronunciation

01:08:12.642 --> 01:08:14.217 align:middle line:90%
starts with coronal.

01:08:14.217 --> 01:08:15.300 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Oh, yeah.

01:08:15.300 --> 01:08:16.920 align:middle line:84%
But in Arabic, I
think it doesn't.

01:08:16.920 --> 01:08:20.279 align:middle line:90%
So maybe you know.

01:08:20.279 --> 01:08:22.740 align:middle line:84%
Do you know know what
"algebra" is in Arabic?

01:08:22.740 --> 01:08:24.428 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: I believe
it's [ARABIC]..

01:08:24.428 --> 01:08:25.470 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: [ARABIC]

01:08:25.470 --> 01:08:26.178 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: [ARABIC]

01:08:26.178 --> 01:08:27.240 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Oh, OK.

01:08:27.240 --> 01:08:28.229 align:middle line:90%
So I got it backwards.

01:08:28.229 --> 01:08:32.100 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, so we kept the "l,"
We didn't do the change.

01:08:32.100 --> 01:08:34.982 align:middle line:84%
So it starts with a "j,"
starts with a coronal.

01:08:34.982 --> 01:08:36.149 align:middle line:90%
But it didn't do the change.

01:08:36.149 --> 01:08:38.819 align:middle line:84%
I wonder whether we
borrowed it before Arabic

01:08:38.819 --> 01:08:40.720 align:middle line:90%
did this sound change.

01:08:40.720 --> 01:08:42.069 align:middle line:90%
I have no idea.

01:08:42.069 --> 01:08:44.540 align:middle line:84%
I don't know enough about
the history of Arabic.

01:08:44.540 --> 01:08:46.270 align:middle line:90%
Cool.

01:08:46.270 --> 01:08:46.770 align:middle line:90%
Yep.

01:08:46.770 --> 01:08:51.609 align:middle line:90%


01:08:51.609 --> 01:08:54.750 align:middle line:90%
All right.

01:08:54.750 --> 01:08:57.180 align:middle line:90%
So this is one rule.

01:08:57.180 --> 01:09:00.180 align:middle line:84%
It's a very common rule,
where one sound becomes more

01:09:00.180 --> 01:09:01.290 align:middle line:90%
like another sound.

01:09:01.290 --> 01:09:03.790 align:middle line:90%
It's called assimilation.

01:09:03.790 --> 01:09:06.540 align:middle line:84%
And in this
particular case, it's

01:09:06.540 --> 01:09:09.180 align:middle line:84%
what's sometimes called
total assimilation, where

01:09:09.180 --> 01:09:12.540 align:middle line:84%
one sound just becomes a
complete copy of another sound.

01:09:12.540 --> 01:09:15.720 align:middle line:84%
We'll see other cases
where one sound just

01:09:15.720 --> 01:09:20.100 align:middle line:84%
changes in ways that
resemble a nearby sound.

01:09:20.100 --> 01:09:21.479 align:middle line:90%
Why do you do assimilation?

01:09:21.479 --> 01:09:23.470 align:middle line:84%
Languages do assimilation
all the time.

01:09:23.470 --> 01:09:27.090 align:middle line:84%
And it's not hard to have
pretheoretical notions

01:09:27.090 --> 01:09:28.350 align:middle line:90%
about why, right?

01:09:28.350 --> 01:09:32.160 align:middle line:84%
You're saving wear and
tear on your articulators.

01:09:32.160 --> 01:09:34.920 align:middle line:84%
So" making it so that your
articulators-- your tongue,

01:09:34.920 --> 01:09:40.770 align:middle line:84%
your lips, your velum don't have
to do as much moving around.

01:09:40.770 --> 01:09:45.342 align:middle line:84%
Things don't move as far
or don't move at all.

01:09:45.342 --> 01:09:47.550 align:middle line:84%
It probably also makes
perception easier in the sense

01:09:47.550 --> 01:09:53.850 align:middle line:84%
that if you're speaking
Arabic and instead of an "l"--

01:09:53.850 --> 01:09:56.490 align:middle line:90%
so let's take the sun.

01:09:56.490 --> 01:09:59.190 align:middle line:90%


01:09:59.190 --> 01:10:03.380 align:middle line:84%
If you know that the word
for "sun" is [ARABIC],,

01:10:03.380 --> 01:10:07.730 align:middle line:84%
and you've got this
thing before it,

01:10:07.730 --> 01:10:09.770 align:middle line:84%
basically, it's
as though you only

01:10:09.770 --> 01:10:12.080 align:middle line:84%
need to give your hearer
enough information

01:10:12.080 --> 01:10:13.820 align:middle line:84%
to help them to
know that they're

01:10:13.820 --> 01:10:17.180 align:middle line:84%
hearing a definite article
before the word for sun.

01:10:17.180 --> 01:10:20.270 align:middle line:84%
And Arabic has
decided it's enough

01:10:20.270 --> 01:10:25.430 align:middle line:84%
if you hear the glottal stop and
the vowel, and then a coronal.

01:10:25.430 --> 01:10:27.980 align:middle line:84%
Underlyingly, that
coronal, maybe, is an "l."

01:10:27.980 --> 01:10:29.510 align:middle line:84%
But as long as we
keep it coronal,

01:10:29.510 --> 01:10:32.550 align:middle line:84%
we've given the hearer enough
that they get to hear it.

01:10:32.550 --> 01:10:35.720 align:middle line:84%
And in fact, we give
the hearer, in a sense,

01:10:35.720 --> 01:10:39.140 align:middle line:84%
two shots at hearing the
beginning of the word for sun.

01:10:39.140 --> 01:10:42.920 align:middle line:90%
So we'll lengthen that sound.

01:10:42.920 --> 01:10:45.350 align:middle line:90%
We'll pronounce it twice.

01:10:45.350 --> 01:10:49.610 align:middle line:84%
So we're redistributing
the signal, in a way,

01:10:49.610 --> 01:10:53.428 align:middle line:84%
to the hearer to make
their life arguably easier,

01:10:53.428 --> 01:10:55.970 align:middle line:84%
giving up some of what makes
the definite article distinctive

01:10:55.970 --> 01:10:58.550 align:middle line:84%
in order to emphasize
some of the beginning

01:10:58.550 --> 01:10:59.390 align:middle line:90%
of the word for sun.

01:10:59.390 --> 01:11:01.340 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:11:01.340 --> 01:11:02.820 align:middle line:90%
Questions about any of this?

01:11:02.820 --> 01:11:03.320 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

01:11:03.320 --> 01:11:04.778 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: So would
you say generally

01:11:04.778 --> 01:11:07.980 align:middle line:84%
that the focus is just to
facilitate the pronunciation

01:11:07.980 --> 01:11:09.700 align:middle line:90%
of the word easier.

01:11:09.700 --> 01:11:12.340 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: So
I was trying to float

01:11:12.340 --> 01:11:15.520 align:middle line:84%
the idea that it's partly to
make the pronunciation easier.

01:11:15.520 --> 01:11:17.860 align:middle line:84%
It's so that your
articulators are not having

01:11:17.860 --> 01:11:20.110 align:middle line:90%
to move as fast or as far.

01:11:20.110 --> 01:11:24.130 align:middle line:84%
But it might also make
perception, in some ways,

01:11:24.130 --> 01:11:26.240 align:middle line:90%
easier in a case like this.

01:11:26.240 --> 01:11:30.640 align:middle line:84%
So the idea is we'll give up
some of the special properties

01:11:30.640 --> 01:11:34.450 align:middle line:84%
of the definite article in
favor of emphasizing or holding

01:11:34.450 --> 01:11:38.590 align:middle line:84%
for longer the special
properties of the word for sun,

01:11:38.590 --> 01:11:40.060 align:middle line:84%
or whatever, the
consonant that's

01:11:40.060 --> 01:11:44.260 align:middle line:84%
at the beginning of
the following noun.

01:11:44.260 --> 01:11:47.440 align:middle line:84%
So we'll emphasize
some parts of the--

01:11:47.440 --> 01:11:49.510 align:middle line:84%
in some ways, the least
predictable part of this.

01:11:49.510 --> 01:11:51.635 align:middle line:84%
You presumably hear definite
articles all the time.

01:11:51.635 --> 01:11:55.000 align:middle line:84%
It's not so important that you
get lots of clues to the fact

01:11:55.000 --> 01:11:56.440 align:middle line:90%
that this is a definite article.

01:11:56.440 --> 01:11:58.000 align:middle line:90%
The nouns are going to vary.

01:11:58.000 --> 01:12:02.300 align:middle line:84%
You'll hear more different
kinds of nouns, yeah.

01:12:02.300 --> 01:12:03.020 align:middle line:90%
Yep.

01:12:03.020 --> 01:12:03.997 align:middle line:90%
OK, cool.

01:12:03.997 --> 01:12:05.580 align:middle line:84%
This is probably a
good place to stop.

01:12:05.580 --> 01:12:06.372 align:middle line:90%
So let's stop here.

01:12:06.372 --> 01:12:08.250 align:middle line:90%
And have a good weekend.

01:12:08.250 --> 01:12:11.680 align:middle line:90%
And I'll see you guys next week.

01:12:11.680 --> 01:12:19.000 align:middle line:90%